WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.920
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.930
where we discuss science,
and science based tools

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.963
for everyday life.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.970
I'm Andrew Huberman and I'm a professor

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.100
of neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.920
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.700
Today we are discussing memory.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.380
In particular, how to improve your memory.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.350
Now the study of memory
is one that dates back

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.400
many decades, and by now there's
a pretty good understanding

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.900
of how memories are formed in the brain.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.107
The different structures involved

00:00:29.107 --> 00:00:31.240
and some of the neuro chemicals involved.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.670
And we will talk about some of that today.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.740
Often overlooked,
however, is that memories

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.620
are not just about learning.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.200
Memories are also about
placing your entire life

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.640
into a context.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.887
And that's because what's
really special about the brain

00:00:43.887 --> 00:00:46.080
and in particular the human brain,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.520
is its ability to place
events in the context

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.180
of past events, the present,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.700
and future events.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.010
And sometimes even combinations
of the past and present.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.500
Or present and future and so on.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.320
So when we talk about memory
what we're really talking about

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.070
is how your immediate experiences relate

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.240
to previous and future experiences.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.640
Today I'm going to make clear how

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.350
that process occurs.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.560
Even if you don't have a background

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.240
in biology or psychology, I promise

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.620
to put it into language that anyone

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.340
can access and understand.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.880
And we are going to talk about

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.740
the science that points to specific tools

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.430
for enhancing learning and memory.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.220
We're also going to talk about unlearning

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.150
and forgetting.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.620
There are of course incidences in which

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.410
we would like to forget things.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.690
And that too is a biological process

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.080
for which great tools exist.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.350
To, for instance, eliminate
or at least reduce

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.630
the emotional load of
a previous experience

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.020
that you really did not like,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.040
or that perhaps even was traumatic to you.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.930
So today you're going to learn

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.370
about the systems in the brain and body

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.360
that establish memories.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.380
You're going to learn why certain memories

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.340
are easier to form than others.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.660
And I'm going to talk about specific tools

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.960
that are grounded in not just one,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.890
not just a dozen, but
well over 100 studies

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.760
in animals and humans that point

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.580
to specific protocols that you can use

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.960
in order to stamp down learning

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.010
of particular things more easily.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.650
And you can also leverage
that same knowledge

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.310
to better forget or unload
the emotional weight

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.570
of experiences that you did not like.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.380
We're also going to
discuss topics like deja vu

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.080
and photographic memory.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.480
And for those of you that do not

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.020
have a photographic memory,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.680
and I should point out that I do not have

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.530
a photographic memory, either.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.520
Well, you will learn how to use

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.910
your visual system in
order to better learn

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.880
visual and auditory information.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.610
There are protocols to do this

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.130
grounded in excellent
peer reviewed research.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.980
So while you may not have
a true photographic memory,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.120
by the end of the episode you will

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.690
have tools in hand, or I should say,

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.800
tools in mind or in eyes and mind,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.370
to be able to encode and
remember specific events

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.380
better than you would otherwise.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.920
Before we begin I would like to emphasize

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.770
that this podcast is
separate from my teaching

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.410
and research roles at Stanford.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.270
It is, however, part
of my desire and effort

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.710
to bring zero cost to consumer
information about science

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.500
and science related tools
to the general public.

