00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.380
Okay, let's talk about memory.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.890
And let's talk about how to get better

00:07:57.890 --> 00:07:59.460
at remembering things.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:01.260
Now in order to address
both of those things

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.510
we need to do a little bit
of brain science 101 review.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.550
And I promise this will
only take two minutes.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.920
And I promise that even if
you don't have a background

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.673
in biology, it will make sense.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.823
We are constantly being
bombarded with physical stimuli.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.440
Patterns of touch on our skin,

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.130
light to our eyes, light to
our skin, for that matter.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:23.880
Smells, tastes, and sound waves.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.650
In fact, if you can hear
me saying this right now,

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.050
well, that's the
consequence of sound waves

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.140
arriving into your ears
through headphones,

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:32.720
a computer, or some other speaker device.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.600
Each one of and all of
those sensory stimuli

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.770
are converted into electricity

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.440
and chemical signals by your
so-called nervous system.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:41.620
Your brain, your spinal cord,

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:43.900
and all their connections
with the organs of the body.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.600
And all the connections
of your organs of the body

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.490
back to your brain and spinal cord.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:50.660
One of the primary jobs of
your nervous system, in fact,

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.060
is to convert physical events in the world

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.950
that are non-negotiable, right?

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:56.730
Photons of light are photons of light.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:57.850
Sound waves are sound waves.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.170
There's no changing that.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.160
But your nervous system does change that.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.330
It converts those things
into electrical signals

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.090
and chemical signals which are

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.220
the language of your nervous system.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:09.660
Now just because you're being bombarded

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:10.737
with all this sensory information

00:09:10.737 --> 00:09:12.880
and it's being converted into a language

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:14.860
that neurons and the rest
of your nervous system

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:18.410
can understand, does not mean
that you are aware of it all.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:20.940
In fact, you are only going to perceive

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.800
a small amount of that
sensory information.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.440
For instance, if you can
hear me speaking right now

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.690
you are perceiving my
voice but you are also,

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.640
most likely, neglecting the feeling

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.010
of the contact of your
skin with whichever surface

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.780
you happen to be sitting or standing on.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.780
So it is only by perceiving a subset,

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.630
a small fraction of the sensory events

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.650
in our environment, that we can make sense

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:44.483
of the world around us.

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:46.320
Otherwise we would just be overwhelmed

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.660
with all the things that are happening

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.520
in any one given moment.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:53.280
Now memory is simply a bias in which

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.240
perceptions will be replayed
again in the future.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.020
Anytime you experience something,

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.100
that is the consequence of specific chains

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.390
of neurons, that we call neural
circuits, being activated.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:07.580
And memory is simply a
bias in the likelihood

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:12.120
that specific chain of neurons
will be activated again.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.490
So for instance, if you
can remember your name

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.480
and I certainly hope that you can,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:18.970
well, that means that
there are specific chains

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:22.160
of neurons in your brain
that represent your name

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.250
and when those neurons
connect with one another

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.410
and communicate electrically
with one another

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.710
in a particular sequence,
you remember your name.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:34.250
Were that particular chain
of neurons to be disrupted,

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.450
you would not be able
to remember your name.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.010
Now this might seem immensely simple,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.840
but it raises this really
interesting question

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.990
which we've talked about before.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.110
Which is, why do we
remember certain things

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:45.943
and not others?

00:10:45.943 --> 00:10:49.070
Because according to what I've just said,

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:50.180
as you go through life,

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:51.910
you're experiencing things all the time.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:54.890
You're constantly being
bombarded with sensory stimuli.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.920
Some of those sensory
stimuli you perceive,

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.290
and only some of those perceptions

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:02.070
get stamped down as memories.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.520
Today I'm going to teach
you how certain things

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:05.880
get stamped down as memories.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.040
And I'm going to teach you how

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.240
to leverage that process in order

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.350
to remember the information
that you want far better.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:13.230
Now, even though I've told you

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.380
that a memory is simply
a bias in the likelihood

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:16.630
that a particular chain of neurons

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:18.810
will be activated in a particular sequence

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:23.300
again and again, it
doesn't operate on its own.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:26.340
In fact, most of what
we remember takes place

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:29.230
in a context of other events.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.830
So for instance, you can most
likely remember your name

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.540
and yet you're probably not thinking about

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.100
when it was that you
first learned your name.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.130
This generally happens when we are very,

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:41.630
very young children.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:45.250
And yet, I'm guessing you
could probably remember

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:47.620
a time when someone
mispronounced your name,

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.310
or made fun of or name.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.180
Or, as the case was for me,

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.890
I got to the 3rd grade and
there were two Andrews.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.930
And sadly for me, I lost the coin flip

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.160
that allowed me to keep Andrew.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.500
And from about 3rd grade
until about 12th grade

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:03.570
people called me Andy.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.030
Which I really did not prefer.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.090
So if you call me Andy in the comments,

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:07.923
I'll delete your comment.

00:12:07.923 --> 00:12:10.000
Just kidding, doesn't bother me that much.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.480
But eventually I reclaimed
Andrew as my name.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.490
Well, it was mine to
begin with and throughout,

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:18.350
but I started going by Andrew again.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:19.183
Why do I say this?

00:12:19.183 --> 00:12:22.170
Well, there's a whole
context to my name for me.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.197
And there may or not be a whole context

00:12:24.197 --> 00:12:25.660
to your name for you.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.020
But presumably, if you asked your parents

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.000
why they named you your given name,

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.980
you'll get a context, etc.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.830
That context reflects the activation

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:35.680
of other neural circuits that are also

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.340
related to other events in your life.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:40.530
Not just your name, but
probably your siblings names

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:41.570
and who your parents are.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:43.040
And on, and on, and on.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.550
And so, the way memory works is that each

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:47.960
individual thing that we remember

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:49.040
or that we want to remember

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.710
is linked to something by either

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:54.580
a close, a medium, or a
very distant association.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.930
This turns out to be immensely important.

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.190
I know many of you will read

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.550
or will encounter
programs that are designed

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:03.630
to help you enhance your memory.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.690
You know, you have these
phenoms that can remember

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.090
50 names in a room full of people.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.100
Or they can remember a bunch
of names of novel objects

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.000
or maybe even in different languages,

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.550
and often times that's
done by association.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:18.070
So people will come up
with little mental tricks

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.290
to either link the sound of a word

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:22.960
or the meaning of a word in some way

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:23.900
that's meaningful for them

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.550
and will enhance their memory.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.130
That can be done and is
impressive when we see it

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.370
and for those of you who can
do that, congratulations.

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:32.090
Most of us can't do that,

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:35.270
or at least it requires a
lot of effort and training.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:37.560
However, there are things that we can do

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.580
that leverage the natural biology

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:41.430
of our nervous system to enhance

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:43.750
learning and memory of
particular perceptions,

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.940
and particular information.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:47.150
Let's first just talk about

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.920
the most basic ways that we
learn and remember things

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.210
and how to improve learning and memory.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.170
And the most basic one is repetition.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.550
Now the study of memory and the role

00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:59.050
of repetition actually dates back

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.690
to the late 1800s, early 1900s

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:03.410
when Ebbinghaus developed

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:06.060
the first so-called learning curves.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:08.870
Now learning curves
are simply what results

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.060
when you quantify how many repetitions

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:15.130
of something are required in
order to remember something.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:18.010
In fact, it's been said
that Ebbinghaus liberated

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.600
the understanding of learning
from the philosophers

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.440
by generating these learning curves.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:23.300
What do we mean by that?

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.000
Well, before Ebbinghaus came along,

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.340
learning and memory were thought

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.683
to be philosophical ideas.

00:14:31.531 --> 00:14:34.080
Ebbinghaus came along and said, well,

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.020
let's actually take some measurements.

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.250
Let's measure how well I can remember

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.610
a sequence of words or
a sequence of numbers

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:41.700
if I just repeat them.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:43.120
So what Ebbinghaus did is he would

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.480
take a sequence of numbers,

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:47.730
or words on a page and he would read them.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:50.790
And then he would take a
separate sheet of paper.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:52.760
And we have to presume he didn't cheat,

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:54.420
and he would write down as many of them

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:55.850
as he could and he would try and keep

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:57.200
them in the same sequence.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.010
Then he would compare to the original list

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:00.820
and he would see how many errors he made.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:02.260
And he would do this over, and over,

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:03.370
and over again.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:04.763
And as you would expect,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.650
early in the training and the learning

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:10.220
it took a lot more repetitions

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:11.390
to get the sequence correct.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:13.940
And over time, it took fewer sequences.

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:15.610
And he referred to that difference

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.070
in the initial number of repetitions

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:20.020
that he had to perform versus

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.700
the later number of repetitions he had

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:25.170
to perform as a so-called savings.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.980
So he literally thought of the brain

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:30.690
as having to generate a kind
of a currency of effort.

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:34.030
And he talked about
savings as the reduction

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.500
in the amount of effort that he had

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.780
to put forward in order
to learn information.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.000
And what he got was a learning curve.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.680
And you can imagine what that
learning curve looked like.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.620
It had a very sharp peak at the beginning

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.087
that dropped off over time.

00:15:44.087 --> 00:15:45.560
And of course, he remembered all this

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:47.290
meaningless information.

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:48.870
But even though the information might

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.700
have been meaningless,
the experiment itself

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:54.900
and what Ebbinghaus demonstrated
was immensely meaningful.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.810
Because what it said
was that with repetition

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:01.630
we can activate particular
sequences of neurons

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:04.640
and that repeated activation lays down

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:06.120
what we call a memory.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.840
And that might all seem like a big duh,

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:10.000
but prior to Ebbinghaus,
none of that was known.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.180
Now, I should also say Ebbinghaus,

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.570
because of when he was alive,

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:14.443
was not aware of these things

00:16:14.443 --> 00:16:16.240
that we called neural circuits.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.390
It was in 1906 that Golgi
and Cajal got the Nobel prize

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:22.860
for actually showing that
neurons are independent cells

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.040
connected by synapses, these
little gaps between them

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:26.260
where they communicate.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:27.830
So he may have been aware of that,

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.180
but the whole notion of neural circuits

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:30.480
hadn't really come about.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.800
Nevertheless, what the
Ebbinghaus learning curves

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:36.380
really established was
that sheer repetition,

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:39.260
just repeating things over
and over and over again

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.540
is sufficient to learn.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.590
Something that no doubt
had been observed before

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.890
but had never been formally quantified.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:50.290
Now, if we look at that result,

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:52.300
there's something really
important that lies

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:55.560
a little bit cryptic, that's
not so obvious to most people.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:58.940
Which is, the information
that he was trying to learn

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.480
wasn't any more
interesting the second time

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.710
than it was the first, probably
it was even less interesting

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.950
and less and less interesting
with each repetition.

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.170
And yet it was sheer repetition

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.950
that allowed him to remember.

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:15.060
Now sometime later in
the early to mid 1920s,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.530
a psychologist in Canada named Donald Hebb

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:20.090
came up with what was
called Hebb's postulate.

00:17:20.090 --> 00:17:23.090
And Hebb's postulate, broadly speaking,

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:26.950
is this idea that if a sequence of neurons

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:31.530
is active at the same time,
or at roughly the same time,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:33.230
that would lead to a strengthening

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.410
of a connections between those neurons.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.510
And many, many decades
of experimentation later

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.083
we now know that postulate to be true.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:46.460
Neurons themselves are not
smart, they don't have knowledge.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:48.490
So every memory is the consequence,

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:51.130
as I told you before, of
the repeated activation

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.360
of a particular chain of neurons.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.680
And what Ebbinghaus
showed through repetition

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.240
and what Donald Hebb proposed
and was eventually verified

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:02.370
through experimentation
on animals and humans

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:06.520
was that if you encourage
the co-activation of neurons.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.824
Meaning have neurons fire
at roughly the same time,

00:18:10.824 --> 00:18:13.000
they will strengthen their connections.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.970
It leads to a bias in the probability

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.783
that those neurons will be active again.

00:18:16.783 --> 00:18:19.260
Now, this is vitally
important because nowadays

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:22.810
we hear a lot about how
memories are the consequence

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.500
of new neurons added into the brain.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.380
Or that every time you learn something,

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:30.810
a new connection in your brain forms.

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:31.850
Well, sorry to break it to you,

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:34.130
but that's simply not the case.

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:36.400
Most of the time, and I
want to emphasize most,

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:38.980
not all but most of the
time when we learn something

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:42.440
it's because existing
neurons, not new neurons,

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.510
but existing neurons
strengthen their connections

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.980
through co-activation
over and over and over.

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:53.090
Through repetition or, and
this is a very important or,

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:58.090
or through very strong
activation once and only once.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:18:59.940
In fact, there's something
called one trial learning

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:02.010
whereby we experience something

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:05.350
and we will remember that thing forever.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:08.120
This is often most associated
with negative events,

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:10.040
and I'll explain why in a few minutes.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:12.970
But it can also be associated
with positive events.

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:17.000
Like the first time you
saw your romantic partner.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.690
Or something that happened
with that romantic partner.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:22.430
Or the first time that you saw your child.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:25.100
Or any other positive
event, as well as any other

00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:26.870
extremely negative event.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:29.600
So again, both repetition, and I guess

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.940
we could label it intensity.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.660
But what we really mean
when we say intensity

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.510
is strong activation
of neurons can lay down

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.870
these traces, these circuits
that are far more likely

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:43.880
to be active again, than had
there not been repetition

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:47.250
or not some strong
activation of those circuits.

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:50.410
So with that in mind, let's
return to the original

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.770
contrarian question that I raised before.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.800
Which is, why do we remember anything?

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.230
Everyday you wake up, your neurons

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.180
in your brain and body are active.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:01.820
Different neural circuits are active.

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:04.180
And yet, you only
remember a small fraction

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:05.570
of the things that happen each day.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.250
And yet, you retain a lot of information

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:10.620
from previous days and the
days before those and so on.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:14.290
It is only with a lot of repetition

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:17.140
or with extremely strong activation

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:20.080
of a given neural circuit that
we will create new memories.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:21.850
And so in a few minutes I'll explain how

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:24.040
to get extremely strong activation

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.020
of particular neural circuits.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:27.400
Repetition is pretty obvious.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:29.120
Repetition is repetition.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.660
But in a few minutes I'll illustrate

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:33.570
a whole set of experiments and a whole set

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:36.230
of tools that point to how you can get

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:39.590
extra strong activation
of a given neural circuit

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:41.220
as it relates to learning so that you will

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:44.040
remember that information,
perhaps not just

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:45.880
with one trial of learning,

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.520
but certainly with far fewer repetitions

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.420
than would be required otherwise.

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:52.220
Before we go any further I want to preface

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:53.060
the discussion by saying

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.060
that there are a lot of
different kinds of memory.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.720
In fact, were you to take a voyage

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:00.210
into the neuroscience, and
or psychology of memory

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.810
you would find an immense number

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.030
of different terms to describe

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.930
the immense number of different types

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:07.920
of memory that researchers focus on.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.380
But for the sake of today's discussion,

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:11.800
I really just want to
focus on short term memory,

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.450
medium term memory, and longterm memory.

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:16.370
And while there is still debate,

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:18.530
as is always the case
with scientists, frankly,

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:20.010
about the exact divisions between

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.160
short term, medium, and longterm memory,

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.410
we can broadly define short term memory

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:26.780
and longterm memory.

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:28.650
And we can describe a
couple different types

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:30.640
of those that I think you can relate to

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.050
in your everyday life.

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:33.700
The most common form of short term memory

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.220
that we're going to focus
on is called working memory.

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:38.690
Working memory is your ability to keep

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.920
a chain of numbers in mind
for some period of time

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:44.220
but the expectation really isn't

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.830
that you would remember
those numbers the next day

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:48.260
and certainly not the next week.

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:49.820
So a good example would be a phone number.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:51.350
If I were to tell ya a phone number,

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:54.957
493-2938, well you could
probably remember it.

00:21:54.957 --> 00:21:57.010
493-2938.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:59.470
But if I came back tomorrow and asked you

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:00.740
to repeat that chain of numbers,

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:02.470
most likely you would not.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:05.210
Unless, of course, we
used a particular tool

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:07.220
to stamp down that memory into your mind

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:10.090
and commit it to longterm memory.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:11.620
Now of course, in this day in age,

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.140
most people have phone numbers
programmed into their phone

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.670
and they don't really have to
remember the exact numbers.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:20.940
It's usually done by contact
identity and so forth.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:22.700
So a different example that some of you

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:24.100
are probably more familiar with

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.930
would be those security codes.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:28.210
So you try and log unto
an app or a website

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:29.900
and it asks you for a security code

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:31.600
that's been sent to your text messages

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.380
and you can either plug that in directly

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.439
in some cases, or you have to remember

00:22:35.439 --> 00:22:37.148
that short sequence of anywhere usually

00:22:37.148 --> 00:22:38.820
from six to seven,
sometimes eight numbers.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.900
Your ability to do that,
to switch back and forth

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:45.600
between web pages or apps
and plug in that number

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:47.820
by remembering the sequence
and plugging it in,

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.840
by texting or keying
it in on your keyboard,

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:53.150
that's a really good
example of working memory.

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:54.660
Longterm memory, of the sort that we're

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.470
going to be talking a lot about today

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:58.590
is your ability to commit certain patterns

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:00.860
of information, either
cognitive information

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:02.480
or motor information.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:03.880
Right, the ability to move your limbs

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:05.600
in a particular sequence.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:07.053
Over long periods of time.

00:23:07.053 --> 00:23:09.360
Such that you could remember it a day,

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.100
or a week, or a month,
or maybe even a year

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:12.920
or several years later.

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:14.640
So we've got short term
memory and longterm memory.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:15.950
And we've got this working memory

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:18.180
which is sort of keeping something online

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:20.040
but then discarding okay.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:21.140
Not online on a computer,

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:22.590
but online within your brain.

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:25.430
There are also two major
categories of memory

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:26.580
that I'd like you to know about.

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:28.510
One is explicit memory.

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:30.760
So this is not necessarily explicit

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:33.010
of the sort that you're
used to thinking about.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:36.470
But rather the fact that you
can declare you know something.

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.540
So you have an explicit
memory of your name.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.770
Presumably you have an explicit memory

00:23:41.770 --> 00:23:44.360
of the house or the apartment
that you grew up in.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:46.730
You know something and
you know you know it.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:47.740
And you can declare it.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:49.400
So I can ask you, what was the color

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:51.830
of the first car that you owned?

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:56.700
Or what is the color of your
romantic partner's hair?

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.176
These sorts of things.

00:23:58.176 --> 00:23:59.770
That's an explicit declarative memory.

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:04.130
But you also have explicit
procedural memories.

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.350
Now procedural memories,
as the name suggests,

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:08.647
involve action sequences.

00:24:08.647 --> 00:24:11.660
The simplest one, it's
almost ridiculously simple,

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:12.493
is walking.

00:24:12.493 --> 00:24:14.560
If I say, how is it that you walk

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:15.830
from one room to the other?

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:17.510
You'd probably say,
well, I go that direction

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:18.343
and then I turn left.

00:24:18.343 --> 00:24:19.176
I say, no, no, no.

00:24:19.176 --> 00:24:20.507
How is it exactly that you do it?

00:24:20.507 --> 00:24:22.270
You say, well, I move my left foot,

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.120
then my right foot, then my left foot.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:25.900
And you could describe that.

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:28.000
So it's an explicit procedural memory.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.180
So much so that if you were going

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:31.620
to teach a young toddler how to walk,

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.900
you would probably say
okay, good, good, try.

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:36.270
Okay, then you know, probably
that's going to be pre-language

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:37.650
for the toddler.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:39.240
But you're going to encourage them

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:40.620
to move one leg then the other.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.130
And you're going to
encourage and reward them

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:44.750
for moving one leg then the other.

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:46.210
Because you have an explicit

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.020
procedural memory of how to walk.

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:50.620
Okay, almost ridiculously simple.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.710
Maybe even truly ridiculously simple,

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.660
but nonetheless, when you think about it

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:56.680
in the context of neural
circuits and neural firing,

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:58.280
pretty amazing.

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:01.610
Even more amazing is the fact that all

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:05.160
explicit memories, both declarative

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:07.900
and procedural explicit memories

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:12.000
can be moved from explicit to implicit.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.020
What do I mean by that?

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.570
Well, in the example of walking

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:16.640
you might have chuckled a little bit

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.210
or kind of shook your head and said,

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:19.780
this is a ridiculous thing to ask.

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:21.220
How do I walk from one room to the next?

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:22.053
I just walk.

00:25:22.053 --> 00:25:22.950
I just do it.

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:25.010
Ah, well, what is just do it?

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.420
What it is, is that you have
an implicit understanding.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:31.710
Meaning your nervous
system knows how to walk

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:35.500
without you actually having to think about

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:37.140
what you know about how to walk.

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:38.440
You just get up out of your chair

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:40.503
or you get up out of bed and you walk.

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.300
In the brain you have a structure.

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:46.000
In fact, you have one on
each side of your brain.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:47.490
It's called the hippocampus.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:49.910
The hippocampus literally means seahorse.

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:52.840
Anatomists like to name
brain structures after things

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:55.520
that they think those
brain structures resemble.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.150
When I look at the hippocampus,

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:59.010
frankly, it doesn't look like a seahorse.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.470
Which either reflects
my lack of understanding

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.840
of what a seahorse really
looks like, a visual deficit,

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:05.500
or I think it's fair to say

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:07.350
that those anatomists
were using a little bit

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:10.417
of creative elaboration
when thinking about

00:26:10.417 --> 00:26:12.270
what the hippocampus looks like.

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:15.400
Nonetheless, it is a curved structure.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.220
It has many layers.

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:19.457
It's been described by my
colleague Robert Sapolsky

00:26:19.457 --> 00:26:22.660
and by others as looking
more like a jelly roll

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:25.760
or a cinnamon roll, is
what it looks like to me.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:28.040
And if you were to take one cinnamon roll,

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:29.740
chop it down the middle.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:32.100
So now you've got two half cinnamon rolls

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.960
and rather than put them back together

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:35.307
in the configuration they were before,

00:26:35.307 --> 00:26:38.730
you just slide one down so that you've got

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.220
essentially two C's.

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:43.430
Two C-shaped halves of this cinnamon roll

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:45.460
and you push them together, right,

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.220
slightly off set from one another.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.360
Well, that's what the
hippocampus looks like to me.

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:51.590
And I think that's a
far better description

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:53.350
of its actual physical structure.

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:56.610
But I guess if you were to
use that physical structure

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:57.820
as the name, well then
you'd have to open up

00:26:57.820 --> 00:26:59.750
a brain atlas and it would be called

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:02.140
two half-C cinnamon rolls
stuffed halfway together.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:02.973
So that's not very good.

00:27:02.973 --> 00:27:04.640
So I guess, seahorse will work.

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.180
Hippocampus is the name of this structure

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:10.230
and it is the site in your brain,

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:13.170
and again, you have one on
each side of your brain,

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:17.510
in which explicit declarative
memories are formed.

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:21.250
It is not where those memories
are stored and maintained.

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:24.003
It is where they are
established in the first place.

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:28.280
In contrast, implicit memories,

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:32.580
the subconscious memories,
are formed and stored

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.360
elsewhere in the brain.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:36.220
Mainly by areas like the cerebellum,

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:37.350
but also the neocortex,

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:39.230
the kind of outer shell of your brain.

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.230
The cerebellum literally means mini-brain.

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:44.190
And it does in fact
look like a mini-brain.

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:45.710
And is in the back of the brain.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:47.960
And the neocortex is the
outer part of the brain

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:49.950
that covers all the other stuff.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:53.450
So, the hippocampus is vitally important

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:56.220
for establishing these
new, declarative memories

00:27:56.220 --> 00:28:00.290
of what you know and
what you know how to do.

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:02.380
Now, in order to really
understand the role

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:04.360
of the hippocampus in
memory, in particular

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:08.040
explicit declarative, and
explicit procedural memory

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:10.380
and to really understand
how that's distinct

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:15.380
from implicit declarative and
implicit procedural memories

00:28:15.571 --> 00:28:17.627
we have to look to a clinical case.

00:28:17.627 --> 00:28:19.330
And the clinical case
that I'm referring to

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:22.180
is a patient who went by the name HM.

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.150
Patient's go by their initials in order

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:27.560
to maintain confidentiality
of their real identity.

00:28:27.560 --> 00:28:31.540
HM had what's called intractable epilepsy.

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:33.940
So he would have these really dramatic,

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:37.150
so-called grand mal
seizures, or drop seizures.

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:39.580
For those of you that know
somebody with epilepsy,

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:43.730
or that have epilepsy, you
might be familiar with this.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:47.130
You can have petite mal seizures,
which are minor seizures.

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:48.590
You can have tonic clonic seizures,

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:50.370
which are sometimes not even detectable.

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:51.850
You can have absent seizures where people

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:53.830
will just stop, it's
almost as if their brain

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:55.660
kind of goes on pause and
they'll just stop there.

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:59.150
It was reported actually that
Einstein had absent seizures.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:00.250
Although I don't know that's ever

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:02.943
really been confirmed neurologically.

00:29:04.690 --> 00:29:06.840
Grand mal seizures are extremely severe

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:08.220
and that's what HM had.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.190
So he could just be going about his day

00:29:10.190 --> 00:29:13.520
and maybe even cooking, or
doing something, driving,

00:29:13.520 --> 00:29:14.780
operating any kind of machinery,

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:16.590
and then all of a sudden
he would just have

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:17.520
a drop seizure.

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:18.970
So he would just physically drop

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:20.400
and go into a grand mal seizure.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:22.210
So convulsing of the whole body,

00:29:22.210 --> 00:29:24.320
loss of consciousness, etc.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:26.370
Or he would feel it coming on.

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:27.720
Often times people with epilepsy

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.730
can feel the epileptic seizure coming on.

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.310
Kind of like a wave from
the back of the brain.

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:33.450
And sometimes they can get

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:35.240
to a safe circumstance, but not always.

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:38.280
And so the frequency and the
intensity of his seizures

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:41.230
were so robust that the neurosurgeons

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.700
and neurologists decided
that they needed to locate

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:46.140
the origin, what they call the foci

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:49.500
of those seizures, and
remove that brain tissue.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.140
Because the way seizures work

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:53.350
is they spread out from that focus,

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.530
or that foci of brain tissue.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:59.020
And unfortunately for HM,

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.260
the focus of his seizures
was the hippocampus.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:04.810
So after a lot of deliberation,

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:06.710
a neurosurgeon, in fact
one of the most famous

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:09.053
neurosurgeons in the world at that time,

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:11.890
made what are called electrolytic lesions,

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:16.320
actually burned out the
hippocampus in the brain of HM.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:20.870
And as a consequence, he
lost all explicit memory.

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:22.120
Now the consequence of this

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:24.140
was that he couldn't exist in normal,

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.120
everyday life, like most people.

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.870
So he had to live mostly, not entirely,

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:31.980
but mostly in a kind of hospital setting.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:34.560
And I've talked to
several people, who have

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.940
I should say, who met HM directly,

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:40.010
because he's no longer alive.

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:44.190
But an interaction with him
might look like the following.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:45.910
He would walk up to you just fine.

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:49.270
You wouldn't know that he
had any kind of brain damage.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:51.500
He could walk fine, he could speak fine.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:53.370
And you'd say, hi, I'm Andrew.

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:56.010
And he'd say, hi, I'm whatever
his name happened to be.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:58.750
He wouldn't say HM, but he'd
probably say his real name.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:02.250
And then perhaps someone new
would walk into the room.

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:03.910
He might turn around, look at that person,

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:05.670
as any of us might do.

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:10.410
Then turn around back to me
and say, hi, what's your name?

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:12.540
And if I were to say, well,
I just told you my name.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:15.021
And you just told me your
name, do you remember that?

00:31:15.021 --> 00:31:16.830
And he'd say, I'm sorry, I
don't remember any of that.

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:17.663
What's your name?

00:31:17.663 --> 00:31:19.240
So you'd go through this
over and over again.

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:22.870
So a complete lack of
explicit declarative memory.

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:26.330
Now he did have some memory for

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:30.240
previous events in his life
that dated way back, okay.

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:33.400
Again, hinting at the idea that memories

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:36.460
are not necessarily
stored in the hippocampus,

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:37.950
they're just formed in the hippocampus.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:39.510
So once they've moved
out of the hippocampus

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:42.300
to other brain areas, he could
still keep those memories.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:44.010
They're in a different
database, if you will.

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.480
They're in a different pattern of firing

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:46.600
of other neural circuits.

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:48.310
But he couldn't form new memories.

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:49.830
Now there's some very important

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:52.710
and interesting twists on what HM could

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:55.210
and could not do in terms
of learning and memory

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:56.850
that teach us a lot about the brain.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:59.130
In fact, I think most
neuroscientists would agree

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:02.160
that this unfortunate
case of HM's epilepsy

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.640
and the subsequent neuro
surgery that he had

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:06.370
taught us much of what we know,

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:07.330
or at least think about,

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:09.710
in terms of human learning and memory.

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:11.620
For instance, as I mentioned before,

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:13.960
he still had implicit knowledge.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:14.850
He knew how to walk.

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:16.530
He knew how to do certain things like

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:17.930
make a cup of coffee.

00:32:17.930 --> 00:32:19.970
He knew the names of people

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:23.950
that he had met much earlier
in his life, and so on.

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:26.090
And yet he couldn't form new memories.

00:32:26.090 --> 00:32:29.420
Now, in violation to that last statement,

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:32.960
there were some elements
of HM's emotionality

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:34.740
that suggests that there was some sort

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:38.100
of residual capacity to
learn new information

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.540
but it wasn't what we normally think of

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:43.130
as explicit declarative
or procedural memory.

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:45.840
For instance, it's been reported

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:47.550
or it's been said, I should say,

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:49.040
because I don't know that the studies

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:51.890
were ever done with intense
physiological measurements,

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:54.860
that if you were to tell HM a joke,

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:58.410
and he thought it was funny,
he would laugh really hard.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:00.209
He liked jokes, so you'd say hey, HM,

00:33:00.209 --> 00:33:01.800
I want to tell you a joke.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:03.980
You tell him a joke and
he'd laugh really hard.

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:06.020
Then you could leave the room, come back,

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:08.010
and tell him the same joke again.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:09.730
Now keep in mind, he did not remember

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:11.750
that you told him the joke previously.

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:14.630
And the second time he would
laugh a little bit less.

00:33:14.630 --> 00:33:17.280
And then you'd leave the
room, come back again.

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:18.928
Say hi, I'm Andrew.

00:33:18.928 --> 00:33:20.467
And he'd say, oh, nice to meet you.

00:33:20.467 --> 00:33:22.040
Because as you know, as you recall,

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:23.070
because you can recall things.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:24.490
But he couldn't recall things.

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:25.820
He didn't know that he just met you.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:27.290
Or at least he couldn't remember it.

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:29.370
You tell him the joke a
third time, or a fourth time,

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:31.810
and with each subsequent
telling of the joke

00:33:31.810 --> 00:33:33.470
he found it a little less funny.

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:36.400
Just as, keep this in mind, folks,

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.220
if you tell a joke and
you get a big laugh,

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:40.660
don't tell it again.

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:42.100
At least not immediately.

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:44.410
Not to the same person or the same crowd

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:46.447
because the second time
it's a little less funny

00:33:46.447 --> 00:33:48.020
and the third time it's
a little less funny.

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:50.250
And that actually has to
do with a whole element

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.410
of dopamine and it's
relationship to surprise.

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.310
And that's the topic of a future podcast

00:33:55.310 --> 00:33:58.450
where we talk all about humor
and novelty in the brain.

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:02.190
But the point being that
certain forms of memory

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:05.040
seem to exist in a kind
of phantom like way

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.440
within HM's brain.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:07.340
What do I mean by that?

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:09.910
Well, this underscores that he had

00:34:09.910 --> 00:34:13.640
an implicit memory of having
heard the joke before.

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:16.760
And it suggests that humor, or
at least what we find funny,

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:19.350
is somehow more related to procedures.

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:21.580
Similar to walking or a motor ability

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:25.650
than it is to this precise
content of that joke.

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:27.510
All right, that's a little
bit of an abstract concept,

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:31.360
but the point is that HM lacked
explicit declarative memory.

00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:34.060
He couldn't tell you
what he had just heard.

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:36.140
He could not learn new information.

00:34:36.140 --> 00:34:38.650
And he couldn't tell
you how to do something

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:40.970
unless he had learned
how to do that something

00:34:40.970 --> 00:34:42.920
many years prior.

00:34:42.920 --> 00:34:45.660
Now, there have been a lot
of other patients besides HM

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:49.240
that have had brain
lesions due to epilepsy,

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:52.020
or I should say due to
surgeries to treat epilepsy,

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:55.620
due to strokes, due to
sadly gunshot wounds

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:59.750
and other forms of what
we call infarcts, infarct.

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:04.460
I-N-F-A-R-C-T, infarct is the word we use

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:07.220
to describe damage to a
particular brain region.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:10.280
And many different patients
with many different patterns

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:13.767
of infarct have taught
us a lot about how memory

00:35:13.767 --> 00:35:15.623
and other aspects of the brain work.

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:21.320
HM really teaches us that what we know

00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:24.220
and what we are able to
do is the consequence

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:26.080
of things that we are aware of

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:28.240
and learnings that have been passed off

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:31.040
into subconscious knowledge,
that our body knows.

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:33.970
Our brain knows, but we don't know exactly

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:35.590
how we know that thing.

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:38.010
And I tell you the
story about HM's ability

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:41.920
to understand a joke, but
that with repeated telling

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:43.680
of the joke it has less and less and less

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:47.140
of an impact in creating
a sense of laughter,

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:50.290
of humor in HM.

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:53.330
Not as just an anecdote
to flesh out his story,

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:56.600
but because emotion itself turns out

00:35:56.600 --> 00:35:59.670
to be the way in which
we can enhance memories

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:01.590
even if those are memories for things

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:04.330
that are not funny, are not intensely sad,

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:08.410
are not immensely happy or don't evoke

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:10.190
a really strong emotional response,

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:13.020
or even any emotional response.

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:15.800
And the reason for that is that emotions,

00:36:15.800 --> 00:36:18.670
just like perception, just like sensation,

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:21.140
are the consequence of
particular neuro chemicals

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:22.720
being present in our brain and body.

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:24.820
And as I'm going to tell you next,

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:26.960
there are particular neuro chemicals

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:29.220
that you can leverage in order to learn

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:32.510
specific information
faster and to remember it

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:34.200
for a much longer period of time,

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:35.610
maybe even forever.

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:37.040
And you can do that by leveraging

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:39.120
the relationship in your nervous system

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:41.500
between your brain and your body.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:44.290
And your body back to your brain.

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:46.860
So let's talk about tools
for enhancing memory.

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:51.380
Now there's one tool that
it's absolutely clear works.

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:53.900
And it's always worked, it works now,

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:56.300
and it will work forever.

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:57.513
And that's repetition.

00:36:58.370 --> 00:37:00.500
The more often that you perform something

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:02.400
or that you recite something,

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:05.720
the more likely you are to
remember it in the future.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:07.410
And while that might seem obvious,

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:09.610
it's worth thinking about what's happening

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:11.030
when you repeat something.

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:12.290
But when I say what's happening,

00:37:12.290 --> 00:37:13.870
I mean at the neural level.

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:16.040
What's happening is
that you're encouraging

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:18.930
the firing of particular chains of neurons

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:21.220
that reside in a
particular circuit, right.

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:23.110
So a particular sequence
of neurons playing

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:26.160
neuron A, B, C, D played
in that particular sequence

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:27.950
over and over and over again.

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:31.350
And with more repetitions,
you get more strengthening

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:33.340
of those nerve connections.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:37.740
Now, repetition works but
the problem for most people

00:37:37.740 --> 00:37:40.060
is they either don't have the patience,

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:40.910
they don't have the time,

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:42.460
and sometimes they literally
don't have the time

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:44.140
because they've got a deadline

00:37:44.140 --> 00:37:47.260
on something that they're
trying to remember and learn.

00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:49.430
Or they simply would like to be able

00:37:49.430 --> 00:37:51.790
to remember things better in general

00:37:51.790 --> 00:37:53.860
and remember them more quickly.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:57.680
This process of accelerating
repetition based learning

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.720
so that your learning curve doesn't go

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:02.630
from having to perform
something 1,000 times

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:06.360
and then gradually over time
it's 1,000, 750 times a day,

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:08.660
500 times a day, 300 times a day,

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:10.430
and down to no repetitions, right?

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:12.680
You can just perform
that thing the first time

00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:13.830
and every time.

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:16.880
Well, there is a way to shift that curve

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:20.960
so that you can essentially
establish stronger connections

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:22.870
between the neurons that are involved

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:26.840
in generating that memory
or behavior more quickly.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:27.700
How do you do that?

00:38:27.700 --> 00:38:29.570
Well, in order to answer that we have

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:33.230
to look at the beautiful
work of James McGaugh

00:38:33.230 --> 00:38:34.710
and Larry Cahill.

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:36.480
James McGaugh and Larry Cahill did

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:39.270
a number experiments over
several decades really

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:41.490
based on a lot of animal literature,

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:44.180
but mainly focused on humans

00:38:44.180 --> 00:38:47.010
that really established what's required

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:49.630
to get better at remembering things

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:51.670
and to do so very quickly.

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:53.900
I want to talk about one
experiment that they did

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:55.737
that was particularly important.

00:38:55.737 --> 00:38:57.570
And we will provide a link to this paper,

00:38:57.570 --> 00:38:59.000
it's some years old now,

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:00.750
but the results still hold up.

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:02.300
In fact, the results established

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:06.030
an entire field of memory and
neuroscience and psychology.

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:08.940
What they did is they had human subjects

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:10.920
come into the laboratory and to read

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:14.480
a short paragraph of about 12 sentences.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:17.560
And the key thing is
that some subjects read

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:19.943
a paragraph that was pretty mundane.

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:23.500
The content, the information
within the paragraph

00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:26.360
was all related to the content
of the previous sentence.

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:27.840
So it was a cogent paragraph.

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:31.010
Right, it just wasn't a
meaningless scramble of words.

00:39:31.010 --> 00:39:35.180
But it described a kind of
mundane set of circumstances.

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:36.800
Maybe it would be a story about someone

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:39.550
who walked into a room,
sat down at a desk,

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:41.180
wrote for a little bit, then got up

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:42.013
and had lunch.

00:39:42.013 --> 00:39:44.100
You know, just kind of
mundane information.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:45.750
Not very interesting.

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:48.670
Another group of subjects read also

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:50.730
a 12 sentence paragraph.

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:53.670
But that paragraph included a subset

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:58.100
of sentences that had a lot of
emotionally intense language.

00:39:58.100 --> 00:40:00.560
Or that had language that could evoke

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:02.130
an emotionally intense response

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:03.360
in the person reading it.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:05.460
So it might have talked
about a car accident

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:07.640
or a very intense surgery.

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:09.780
But it also could be positive stuff.

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:12.280
Things like a birthday party,

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:14.000
or a celebration of some other kind.

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:15.600
Or a big sports win.

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.470
So in other words, you have
two conditions of this study.

00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:20.730
People either read a boring paragraph,

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:23.540
or they read a really
emotionally laden paragraph.

00:40:23.540 --> 00:40:25.070
And again, the emotions could either be

00:40:25.070 --> 00:40:27.093
positive or negative emotions.

00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:30.340
Subjects left the laboratory
and sometime later

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:32.090
they were called back to the laboratory

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:34.740
and I should say, at no
point in the experiment

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:36.830
did they know they were
part of a memory experiment.

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:37.840
Okay, they don't even know why

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:39.050
they were reading this paragraph.

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:41.780
They came in either for
class credit or to get paid.

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:42.950
That's typically how these things are done

00:40:42.950 --> 00:40:45.230
on college campuses or elsewhere.

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:46.900
They come back into the lab

00:40:46.900 --> 00:40:49.650
and they would get a pop quiz.

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:52.170
They would be asked to recall the content

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:55.530
of the paragraph that
they had read previously.

00:40:55.530 --> 00:40:58.913
Now as is probably expected,
perhaps even obvious to you,

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:03.720
the subjects that read the
emotionally intense paragraph

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:06.550
remembered far more of the
content of that paragraph

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:08.960
and were far more accurate

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:11.720
in their remembering of that information.

00:41:11.720 --> 00:41:15.490
Now, that particular
finding wasn't very novel.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:17.440
Many people had previously described

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:19.760
how emotionally intense events

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:22.810
are better remembered than
non-emotionally intense events.

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:24.720
In fact, way back in the 1600s

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:26.740
Francis Bacon, who's largely credited

00:41:26.740 --> 00:41:29.370
with developing the scientific method,

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:32.150
said, quote, memory is
assisted by anything

00:41:32.150 --> 00:41:34.330
that makes an impression
on a powerful passion.

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:35.980
Inspiring fear, for example,

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:38.430
or wonder, shame, or joy.

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:40.910
Francis Bacon said that in 1620.

00:41:40.910 --> 00:41:42.750
So Jim McGaugh and Larry Cahill

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:44.800
were certainly not the first

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:47.730
to demonstrate or to conceive of the idea

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:50.100
that emotionally laden experiences

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:53.500
are more easily remembered
than other experiences.

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:55.420
However, what they did next

00:41:55.420 --> 00:41:58.340
was immensely important for
our understanding of memory

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:00.930
and for our building of tools

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:03.450
to enhance learning and memory.

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.280
What they did was they evaluated

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:09.220
the capacity for stress and for particular

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:11.490
neuro chemicals associated with stress

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:14.430
to improve our ability
to learn information.

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:17.650
Not just information that is emotional,

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:19.970
but information of all kinds.

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:21.610
So I'm going to describe
some experiments done

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:23.430
in animal models just very briefly,

00:42:23.430 --> 00:42:26.660
and then experiments
done on humans subjects.

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:28.950
Because McGaugh worked mainly on animals,

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:30.120
also human subjects.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:32.983
Larry Cahill, almost
exclusively on human subjects.

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:36.350
If you take a rat or a mouse

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:39.990
and put it in an arena
where at one location

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:43.310
the animal receives an electrical shock

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:44.670
and then you come back the next day,

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:47.310
you remove the shock evoking device

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:49.520
and you let the animal
move around that arena,

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:51.500
that animal will, quite understandably,

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:54.000
avoid the location where it was shocked.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:56.343
So called conditioned place aversion.

00:42:57.300 --> 00:43:00.680
That affect of avoiding
that particular location

00:43:00.680 --> 00:43:01.810
occurs in one trial.

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:03.960
That's a good example
of one trial learning.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:05.990
So somehow the animal knows

00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:08.200
that it was shocked at that location,

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:09.830
it remembers that.

00:43:09.830 --> 00:43:12.320
It is a hippocampal dependent learning.

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:14.210
So animals that lack a hippocampus

00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:16.290
or who have their
hippocampus pharmacologically

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:19.670
or otherwise incapacitated, will not learn

00:43:19.670 --> 00:43:21.920
that new bit of information.

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:25.160
But for animals that do, they remember it

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.740
after the first time and every time.

00:43:27.740 --> 00:43:31.280
Unless, you are to block the release

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:33.000
of certain chemicals in the brain and body

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:36.310
and the chemicals I'm
referring to are epinephrine,

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:40.120
adrenaline, and to some
extent the corticosterones.

00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:41.760
Things like cortisol.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.410
Now we know that the effect
of getting one trial learning

00:43:44.410 --> 00:43:46.370
somehow involves epinephrine,

00:43:46.370 --> 00:43:49.200
at least in this particular
experimental scenario.

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:52.920
Because if researchers do
the exact same experiment,

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:54.900
and they have done the
exact same experiment,

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:58.430
but they introduce a
pharmacological blocker

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:01.410
of epinephrine, so that
epinephrine is released

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:02.490
in response to the shock,

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:05.240
but it cannot actually
bind to its receptors

00:44:05.240 --> 00:44:07.380
and have all its biological effects,

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:09.330
well then the animal is perfectly happy

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:12.730
to tread back into the area
where it received the shock.

00:44:12.730 --> 00:44:14.310
It's almost as if it didn't know,

00:44:14.310 --> 00:44:17.170
or we have to assume, it didn't remember

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:19.720
that it received the
shock at that location.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:21.430
So it all seems pretty
obvious when you hear it.

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:22.670
Something bad happens in a location,

00:44:22.670 --> 00:44:24.180
you don't go back to that location.

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:26.420
So that's condition place avoidance.

00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:29.200
But it turns out that the
opposite is also true.

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:32.450
Meaning for something called
condition place preference

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:34.810
you can take an animal,
put it into an arena,

00:44:34.810 --> 00:44:38.140
feed it or reward it
some how at one location

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:38.973
in that arena.

00:44:38.973 --> 00:44:41.070
So you can give a hungry rat or mouse food

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:43.420
at one particular location,

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:45.400
take the animal out,
come back the next day.

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:47.180
No food is introduced, but it will go back

00:44:47.180 --> 00:44:49.210
to the location where
it received the food.

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:50.720
Or you can do any variant of this.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:52.260
You can make the arena a little bit chilly

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:54.300
and provide warmth at that location.

00:44:54.300 --> 00:44:57.370
Or you can take a male animal.

00:44:57.370 --> 00:44:59.390
And it turns out male rats and mice

00:44:59.390 --> 00:45:00.560
will mate at any point.

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:02.300
Or a female animal
that's at the particular

00:45:02.300 --> 00:45:04.230
so called receptive
phase of her mating cycle

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:05.850
and give them an opportunity to mate

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:07.630
at a give location, they'll
go back to that location

00:45:07.630 --> 00:45:08.463
and wait away.

00:45:08.463 --> 00:45:09.610
This is perhaps why people go back

00:45:09.610 --> 00:45:11.860
to the same bar, or the seat at the bar,

00:45:11.860 --> 00:45:13.540
or the same restaurant and wait

00:45:13.540 --> 00:45:14.920
because of the one time they, you know,

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:16.290
things worked out for them.

00:45:16.290 --> 00:45:18.280
Whatever the context was.

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:19.893
Condition place preference.

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:25.250
Condition place preference as
with condition place avoidance

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:28.310
depends on the release
of adrenaline, right.

00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:30.230
It's not just about stress.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:32.940
It's about a heightened emotional state

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:34.320
in the brain and body.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:35.510
Okay, this is really important.

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:37.290
It's not just about stress.

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:38.490
You can get one trial learning

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:40.960
for positive events,
condition place preference.

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:45.620
And you can get one trial
learning for negative events.

00:45:45.620 --> 00:45:47.320
Here I say positive and negative,

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:48.810
I'm putting what's called valence on it.

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:50.920
Making a value judgment about whether not

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:52.400
the animal liked it or didn't like it.

00:45:52.400 --> 00:45:54.690
And we have to presume
what the animal liked

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:56.090
or didn't like and how it felt.

00:45:56.090 --> 00:45:59.870
But this turns out all to
be true for humans as well.

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:02.180
We know that because McGaugh and Cahill

00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:04.860
did experiments where they gave people

00:46:04.860 --> 00:46:07.050
a boring paragraph to read

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:09.400
and only a boring paragraph to read.

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:12.350
But one group of subjects was asked

00:46:12.350 --> 00:46:15.030
to read the paragraph and
then to place their arm

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:17.640
into very, very cold water.

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:19.190
In fact, it was ice water.

00:46:19.190 --> 00:46:21.590
We know that placing
one's arm into ice water,

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:25.130
especially if it's up to
the shoulder or near to it,

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:27.540
evokes the release of
adrenaline in the body.

00:46:27.540 --> 00:46:28.810
It's not an enormous release,

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:30.410
but it's a significant increase.

00:46:30.410 --> 00:46:32.960
And yes, they measured adrenaline release.

00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:34.630
In some cases they also
measured for things like

00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:36.450
cortisol, etc.

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:40.290
And what they found is that if one evokes

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:42.530
the release of adrenaline through

00:46:42.530 --> 00:46:45.810
this arm into ice water approach,

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:47.930
the information that they read previously,

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:49.540
just a few minutes before,

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:53.470
was remembered, it was retained as well

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:55.440
as emotionally intense information.

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:57.100
But keep in mind the
information that they read

00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.150
was not interesting at all.

00:46:58.150 --> 00:47:01.020
Or at least, it wasn't emotionally laden.

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:03.850
This had to be the effect
of adrenaline released

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:07.545
into the brain and body, because
if they blocked the release

00:47:07.545 --> 00:47:12.000
or the function of adrenaline in the brain

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:14.670
and or body, they could block this effect.

00:47:14.670 --> 00:47:18.125
Now the biology of
epinephrine and cortisol

00:47:18.125 --> 00:47:18.958
are a little bit complex,

00:47:18.958 --> 00:47:20.877
but there's some nuance there
that's actually interesting

00:47:20.877 --> 00:47:22.070
and important to us.

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:24.740
First of all, adrenaline is released

00:47:24.740 --> 00:47:26.030
in the body and in the brain.

00:47:26.030 --> 00:47:28.090
It's released in the
body from the adrenals.

00:47:28.090 --> 00:47:31.090
Remember, epinephrine and
adrenaline are the same thing.

00:47:31.090 --> 00:47:33.140
Cortisol is also released
from the adrenal glands.

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:35.640
These two little glands
that ride atop our kidneys.

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:37.500
But it can't cross into the brain.

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:39.320
It only has what we
call peripheral effects.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:41.120
Quickening of the heart rate, right?

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:43.680
Changes the patterns of blood flow.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:45.080
Changes our patterns of breathing.

00:47:45.080 --> 00:47:47.830
In general, makes our breathing
more shallow and faster.

00:47:47.830 --> 00:47:50.330
In general makes our heart
beat more quickly, etc.

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:53.300
Within our brain we
have a little brain area

00:47:53.300 --> 00:47:55.650
called locus coeruleus, which
is in the back of the brain.

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:57.400
Which has the opportunity to sprinkler

00:47:57.400 --> 00:47:58.920
the rest of the brain with

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:01.390
the neuromodulator
epinephrine, adrenaline,

00:48:01.390 --> 00:48:04.270
as well as norepinephrine,
a related neuromodulator.

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:07.550
And to essentially wake up or
create a state of alertness

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:08.630
throughout the brain.

00:48:08.630 --> 00:48:10.630
So it's a very general effect.

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:12.870
The reason we have two sites of release

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:16.500
is because these neuro
chemicals do not cross

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:17.940
the blood-brain barrier.

00:48:17.940 --> 00:48:20.550
And so waking up the body with adrenaline

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:22.410
and waking up the brain are two separate,

00:48:22.410 --> 00:48:24.530
so-called parallel phenomena.

00:48:24.530 --> 00:48:27.240
Cortisol can cross the blood-brain barrier

00:48:27.240 --> 00:48:28.620
because it's lipophilic.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:31.680
Meaning it can move through fatty tissue.

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:33.130
And we'll get into the biology of that

00:48:33.130 --> 00:48:34.290
in another episode.

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:36.320
But cortisol in general is released

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:37.960
and has much longer term effects.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:39.680
And as I just told you, can permeate

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:40.750
throughout the brain and body.

00:48:40.750 --> 00:48:42.410
Adrenaline has more local effects.

00:48:42.410 --> 00:48:45.260
Or at least is segregated
between the brain and the body.

00:48:45.260 --> 00:48:47.330
This will turn out to be important later.

00:48:47.330 --> 00:48:49.431
The important thing to keep in mind

00:48:49.431 --> 00:48:51.030
is that it is the emotionality evoked

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:54.595
by an experience, or to be more precise,

00:48:54.595 --> 00:48:57.670
it is the emotional state that you are in

00:48:57.670 --> 00:49:00.980
after you experience something

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:02.840
that dictates whether
or not you will learn it

00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:04.640
quickly or not.

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:06.595
This is absolutely important

00:49:06.595 --> 00:49:08.400
in terms of thinking about tools

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:09.590
to improve your memory.

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:11.360
And no, I am not going to suggest

00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:12.660
that every time you
want to learn something

00:49:12.660 --> 00:49:15.240
you plunge your arm into ice water.

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:16.530
Why won't I suggest that?

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:20.650
Well, it will induce the
release of adrenaline,

00:49:20.650 --> 00:49:23.010
but there are better ways to
get that adrenaline release.

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:25.490
Before I explain exactly
what those tools are,

00:49:25.490 --> 00:49:27.610
I want to tamp down on the biology

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:28.610
of how all this works.

00:49:28.610 --> 00:49:32.030
Because in that understanding
you will have access

00:49:32.030 --> 00:49:35.260
to the best possible tools
to improve your memory.

00:49:35.260 --> 00:49:37.410
First of all, McGaugh and Cahill

00:49:37.410 --> 00:49:38.930
were excellent experimentalists.

00:49:38.930 --> 00:49:42.250
They did not just establish that you could

00:49:42.250 --> 00:49:44.740
quicken the formation of a memory

00:49:44.740 --> 00:49:48.050
by accessing material that
was very emotionally laden

00:49:48.050 --> 00:49:51.150
or creating an emotional,
high adrenaline state

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:54.350
after interacting with some thing.

00:49:54.350 --> 00:49:57.210
Some word, some person, some information.

00:49:57.210 --> 00:49:59.750
They also tested whether
or not that whole effect

00:49:59.750 --> 00:50:03.080
could be blocked by
blocking the emotional state

00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:04.190
or by blocking adrenaline.

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:05.930
So what they did is they
had people read paragraphs

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:09.030
that either had a lot of emotional content

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:10.800
or they had people read paragraphs

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.050
that were pretty boring, but then had them

00:50:13.050 --> 00:50:14.640
put their arm into ice water.

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:16.360
And I should say they
did other experiments too

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:17.430
to increase adrenaline.

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:18.730
There were even some shock experiments

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:20.280
that were done by other groups.

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:21.420
Any number of things to evoke

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:22.253
the release of adrenaline.

00:50:22.253 --> 00:50:24.743
Even people taking drugs
that increase adrenaline.

00:50:25.580 --> 00:50:29.060
But then they also did what are
called blocking experiments.

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:31.390
They did experiments where they had people

00:50:31.390 --> 00:50:33.330
get into a highly emotional state

00:50:33.330 --> 00:50:35.670
from reading highly emotional material,

00:50:35.670 --> 00:50:37.690
or they got people to get
into a highly emotional

00:50:37.690 --> 00:50:39.890
neuro chemical state by
reading boring material

00:50:39.890 --> 00:50:42.530
and then taking a drug
to increase adrenaline,

00:50:42.530 --> 00:50:44.370
or an ice bath, or a shock.

00:50:44.370 --> 00:50:48.430
And then they also administered
a drug called a beta blocker

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:50.890
to block the affect of adrenaline

00:50:50.890 --> 00:50:53.000
and related chemicals
in the brain and body.

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:55.720
And what they found is that even

00:50:55.720 --> 00:50:58.970
if people were exposed to
something really emotional

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:02.240
or had a lot of adrenaline in their system

00:51:02.240 --> 00:51:03.740
because they received a drug to increase

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:04.573
the amount of adrenaline.

00:51:04.573 --> 00:51:06.660
Two manipulations that
normally would increase memory,

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:08.090
keep that in mind.

00:51:08.090 --> 00:51:10.520
If they gave them a beta blocker,

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:13.570
which reduced the response
to that adrenaline, right?

00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:14.880
So no quickening of the heart rate.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:16.890
No quickening of the breathing.

00:51:16.890 --> 00:51:20.100
No increase in the
activity of locus coeruleus

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:22.730
and these kind of wake up
signals to the rest of the brain.

00:51:22.730 --> 00:51:26.040
Well then, the material wasn't
remembered better at all.

00:51:26.040 --> 00:51:27.780
What this tells us is that, yes,

00:51:27.780 --> 00:51:29.110
Francis Bacon was right.

00:51:29.110 --> 00:51:30.580
McGaugh and Cahill were right.

00:51:30.580 --> 00:51:32.700
Hundreds, if not
thousands of philosophers,

00:51:32.700 --> 00:51:35.710
and psychologists, and
neuroscientists were right.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:39.240
In stating and in thinking that

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:42.090
high emotional states
help you learn things.

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:45.030
But what McGaugh and Cahill really showed,

00:51:45.030 --> 00:51:47.030
and what's most important to know,

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:51.870
is that it is the presence
of high adrenaline,

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:54.650
high amounts of
norepinephrine and epinephrine

00:51:54.650 --> 00:51:58.190
and perhaps cortisol as
well, as you'll soon see,

00:51:58.190 --> 00:52:01.973
that allows a memory to
be stamped down quickly.

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:05.420
It is not the emotion.

00:52:05.420 --> 00:52:08.160
It is the neuro chemical
state that you go into

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:10.510
as a consequence of the emotion.

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:11.760
And it's very important to understand

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:13.440
that while those two things are related,

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:15.790
they are not one and the same thing.

00:52:15.790 --> 00:52:20.070
Because what that means
is that were you to evoke

00:52:20.070 --> 00:52:23.710
the release of epinephrine,
norepinephrine, and cortisol

00:52:23.710 --> 00:52:27.130
or even just one or two of those chemicals

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:29.230
after experiencing something,

00:52:29.230 --> 00:52:32.160
you are stamping down the experience

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:33.600
that you just previously had.

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:36.140
Now this is fundamentally important

00:52:36.140 --> 00:52:39.350
and far and away different than the idea

00:52:39.350 --> 00:52:42.890
that we remember things because
they're important to us,

00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:44.270
or because they evoke emotion.

00:52:44.270 --> 00:52:46.460
That's true, but the real reason,

00:52:46.460 --> 00:52:49.600
the neuro chemical reason,
the mechanism behind all that

00:52:49.600 --> 00:52:52.510
is these neuro chemicals have the ability

00:52:52.510 --> 00:52:54.770
to strengthen neural connections

00:52:54.770 --> 00:52:57.490
by making them active just once.

00:52:57.490 --> 00:52:59.220
There's something truly magic about

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:01.610
that neuro chemical cocktail that removes

00:53:01.610 --> 00:53:03.230
the need for repetition.

00:53:03.230 --> 00:53:04.740
Okay, so let's apply this knowledge.

00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:08.290
Let's establish a scientifically
grounded set of tools.

00:53:08.290 --> 00:53:10.930
Meaning tools that take into account

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:13.070
the identity of the neuro chemicals

00:53:13.070 --> 00:53:15.560
that are important for enhancing learning

00:53:15.560 --> 00:53:18.100
and the timing of the
release of those chemicals

00:53:18.100 --> 00:53:20.270
in order to enhance learning.

00:53:20.270 --> 00:53:21.740
When I first learned about the results

00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:25.700
of McGaugh and Cahill,
I was just blown away.

00:53:25.700 --> 00:53:28.353
I was also pretty upset,
but not with them,

00:53:28.353 --> 00:53:29.670
I was upset with myself.

00:53:29.670 --> 00:53:31.210
Because I realized that the way that I had

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:34.500
been approaching learning
and memory was not optimal.

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:37.830
In fact, it was probably
in the opposite direction

00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:40.710
to the enhanced protocol
for learning and memory

00:53:40.710 --> 00:53:43.110
that I'm going to teach you today.

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:44.970
My typical mode of
trying to learn something

00:53:44.970 --> 00:53:48.010
while I was in college, or
while I was in graduate school,

00:53:48.010 --> 00:53:51.220
or as a junior professor,
or a tenured professor

00:53:51.220 --> 00:53:54.010
was to sit down to
whatever it is I was going

00:53:54.010 --> 00:53:56.630
to try and learn, and
perhaps even memorize.

00:53:56.630 --> 00:53:59.090
Or if it was a physical
skill, move to whatever

00:53:59.090 --> 00:54:01.550
environment I was going to
learn that physical skill in,

00:54:01.550 --> 00:54:05.060
and prior to that, to make sure that I was

00:54:05.060 --> 00:54:06.900
hydrated, because that's important to me.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:09.430
And certainly can
contribute to your brain's

00:54:09.430 --> 00:54:12.030
ability to function and your
body's ability to function.

00:54:12.030 --> 00:54:14.110
And general patterns of alertness.

00:54:14.110 --> 00:54:16.140
But also, to caffeinate.

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:19.440
I would have a nice, strong
cup of coffee or espresso.

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:22.060
I would have a nice
strong cup of yerba mate.

00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:27.040
And I still drink coffee or
yerba mate very regularly.

00:54:27.040 --> 00:54:29.010
I drink them in moderation, I think.

00:54:29.010 --> 00:54:30.380
Certainly for me.

00:54:30.380 --> 00:54:33.560
But typically I would drink those things

00:54:33.560 --> 00:54:36.260
before I would engage
in any kind of attempt

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:37.630
to learn or memorize.

00:54:37.630 --> 00:54:39.270
Or to acquire a new skill.

00:54:39.270 --> 00:54:43.180
Now caffeine in the form
of coffee or yerba mate

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:45.730
or any other form of caffeine does create

00:54:45.730 --> 00:54:47.420
a sense of alertness in our brain and body

00:54:47.420 --> 00:54:49.720
and it does that through
two major mechanisms.

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:54.210
The first mechanism is by
blocking the effects of adenosine.

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:55.680
Adenosine is a molecule that builds up

00:54:55.680 --> 00:54:57.780
in the brain and body the
longer that we are awake.

00:54:57.780 --> 00:54:59.630
And it's largely what's responsible

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:02.190
for our feelings of sleepiness and fatigue

00:55:02.190 --> 00:55:04.340
when we've been awake
for a very long time.

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:09.650
Caffeine essentially acts to block

00:55:09.650 --> 00:55:10.670
the effects of adenosine.

00:55:10.670 --> 00:55:13.640
It's a competing agonist,
not to get technical,

00:55:13.640 --> 00:55:16.280
but it binds to the receptor for adenosine

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:18.170
for some period of time
and prevents adenosine

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:20.060
from having its normal pattern of action.

00:55:20.060 --> 00:55:23.840
And thereby reduces our
feelings of fatigue.

00:55:23.840 --> 00:55:26.630
But it also increases state of alertness.

00:55:26.630 --> 00:55:28.850
So while it's reducing fatigue,

00:55:28.850 --> 00:55:31.050
it's also pushing on
neuro chemical systems

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:32.770
in order to directly
increase our alertness.

00:55:32.770 --> 00:55:35.800
And it does that in
large part by increasing

00:55:35.800 --> 00:55:38.530
the transmission of
epinephrine, adrenaline,

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:39.580
in the brain and body.

00:55:39.580 --> 00:55:41.900
It also has this interesting
effect of up regulating

00:55:41.900 --> 00:55:45.780
the number and or efficiency,
or we say the efficacy,

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:48.240
of dopamine receptors.

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:50.780
Such that when dopamine is present,

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:52.710
and is a molecule that
increases motivation,

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:54.750
and craving, and pursuit, that dopamine

00:55:54.750 --> 00:55:57.230
can have a more potent effect
than it would otherwise.

00:55:57.230 --> 00:55:59.290
So caffeine really hits
these three systems.

00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:02.010
It hits other systems too,
but it mainly reduces fatigue

00:56:02.010 --> 00:56:04.840
by reducing adenosine, increases alertness

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:07.250
by increasing epinephrine release,

00:56:07.250 --> 00:56:08.610
or adrenaline release I should say,

00:56:08.610 --> 00:56:10.180
both from the adrenals in your body

00:56:10.180 --> 00:56:12.770
and form locus coeruleus
from within the brain.

00:56:12.770 --> 00:56:16.230
And it can, in parallel
to all that, increase

00:56:16.230 --> 00:56:20.190
the action or the efficacy
of the action of dopamine.

00:56:20.190 --> 00:56:22.820
So my typical way of
approaching learning and memory

00:56:22.820 --> 00:56:25.790
would be to drink some caffeine
and then focus really hard

00:56:25.790 --> 00:56:27.520
on whatever it is that
I'm trying to learn.

00:56:27.520 --> 00:56:29.530
Try and eliminate distractions

00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:31.470
and then hope, hope, hope.

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:33.840
Or try, try, try to
remember that information

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:35.470
as best as I could.

00:56:35.470 --> 00:56:37.710
And frankly, I felt like it
was working pretty well for me.

00:56:37.710 --> 00:56:39.450
And typically, if I leveraged other forms

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:43.150
of pharmacology in order to
enhance learning and memory,

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:47.460
things like Alpha GPC,
or phosphatidyl serine,

00:56:47.460 --> 00:56:50.210
I would do that by taking those things

00:56:50.210 --> 00:56:52.360
before I sat down to learn

00:56:52.360 --> 00:56:54.050
a particular set of information.

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:57.113
Or before I went off to learn
a particular physical skill.

00:56:58.080 --> 00:57:00.300
Now, for those of you out
there listening to this

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:03.160
you're probably thinking, well, okay.

00:57:03.160 --> 00:57:05.410
The results of McGaugh and Cahill pointed

00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:09.355
to the fact that having
adrenaline released after

00:57:09.355 --> 00:57:12.580
learning something enhanced
learning of that thing.

00:57:12.580 --> 00:57:14.650
But a lot of these things like caffeine,

00:57:14.650 --> 00:57:18.690
or Alpha GPC can increase
epinephrine and adrenaline

00:57:18.690 --> 00:57:23.173
or dopamine or other molecules
in the brain and body

00:57:23.173 --> 00:57:25.750
that can enhance memory
for a long period of time.

00:57:25.750 --> 00:57:27.480
So it makes sense to take it first,

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:28.780
or even during learning,

00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:31.050
and then allow that increase to occur.

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:32.143
And the increase will occur over

00:57:32.143 --> 00:57:35.310
a long period of time and will
enhance learning and memory.

00:57:35.310 --> 00:57:38.100
And while that is partially true,

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:39.580
it is not entirely true.

00:57:39.580 --> 00:57:41.840
And it turns out it's not optimal.

00:57:41.840 --> 00:57:44.210
Work that was done by
the McGaugh laboratory,

00:57:44.210 --> 00:57:47.440
and in other laboratories evaluated

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:50.190
the precise temporal relationship

00:57:50.190 --> 00:57:54.110
between neuro chemical
activation of these pathways

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:55.320
and learning and memory.

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:57.570
What they did is they had
animals and or people,

00:57:57.570 --> 00:58:00.020
depending on the experiment, take a drug.

00:58:00.020 --> 00:58:01.540
It could be caffeine.

00:58:01.540 --> 00:58:03.660
It could be in pill form.

00:58:03.660 --> 00:58:06.740
Something that would increase adrenaline

00:58:06.740 --> 00:58:08.910
or related molecules that create

00:58:08.910 --> 00:58:12.160
this state of alertness that
are related to emotionality.

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:14.480
And they had them do it
either an hour before,

00:58:14.480 --> 00:58:17.370
30 minutes before, 10 minutes before,

00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:20.130
or five minutes before learning,

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:23.150
or during the about of learning, right?

00:58:23.150 --> 00:58:24.900
The reading of the
information or the performing

00:58:24.900 --> 00:58:26.260
of the skill that one is trying to learn.

00:58:26.260 --> 00:58:28.870
Or five minutes, 10 minutes, 15 minutes,

00:58:28.870 --> 00:58:30.940
30 minutes, etc. afterwards.

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:33.580
So they looked very
precisely at when exactly

00:58:33.580 --> 00:58:36.000
is best to evoke this adrenaline release.

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:38.300
And it turns out that the best time window

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:40.700
to evoke the release of these chemicals,

00:58:40.700 --> 00:58:43.150
if the goal is to enhance
learning and memory

00:58:43.150 --> 00:58:46.700
of the material is
either immediately after

00:58:46.700 --> 00:58:50.870
or just a few minutes,
five, 10, maybe 15 minutes

00:58:50.870 --> 00:58:53.960
after you're repeating that information.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:55.420
You're trying to learn that information.

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:57.210
Again, this could be cognitive information

00:58:57.210 --> 00:58:59.230
or this could be a physical skill.

00:58:59.230 --> 00:59:01.380
Now this really spits in the face

00:59:01.380 --> 00:59:04.010
of the way that most of us
approach learning and memory.

00:59:04.010 --> 00:59:08.270
Most of us, if we use
stimulants like caffeine

00:59:08.270 --> 00:59:11.190
or Alpha GPC, we're taking those before

00:59:11.190 --> 00:59:14.483
or during an attempt to
learn, not afterwards.

00:59:15.500 --> 00:59:17.810
These results point to the fact that

00:59:17.810 --> 00:59:19.840
it is after the learning and memory

00:59:19.840 --> 00:59:22.180
that you really want to
get that big increase

00:59:22.180 --> 00:59:24.510
in epinephrine and the related molecules

00:59:24.510 --> 00:59:25.920
that will tamp down memory.

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:28.570
So what this means is that
if you are currently using

00:59:28.570 --> 00:59:30.570
caffeine or other compounds,

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:31.870
and we'll talk about what those are

00:59:31.870 --> 00:59:34.653
and safety issues and
so forth in a moment.

00:59:35.530 --> 00:59:37.150
If you're using those compounds in order

00:59:37.150 --> 00:59:38.470
to enhance learning and memory

00:59:38.470 --> 00:59:42.600
by taking them before or
during a learning episode,

00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:44.520
well then I encourage you to try

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:47.380
and take them either late
in the learning episode

00:59:47.380 --> 00:59:49.920
or immediately after the learning episode.

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:51.590
Now given everything I've
told you up until now

00:59:51.590 --> 00:59:53.490
why would I say late
in the learning episode

00:59:53.490 --> 00:59:54.610
or immediately after?

00:59:54.610 --> 00:59:57.190
Well, when you ingest
something by drinking it

00:59:57.190 --> 00:59:58.740
or you take it in capsule form,

00:59:58.740 --> 01:00:00.190
there's a period of time before that gets

01:00:00.190 --> 01:00:01.090
absorbed into the body.

01:00:01.090 --> 01:00:02.920
And different substances,

01:00:02.920 --> 01:00:05.210
such as caffeine, Alpha GPC, etc

01:00:05.210 --> 01:00:08.070
are absorbed from the gut
and into the blood stream

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:10.140
and reach the brain and
trigger these affects

01:00:10.140 --> 01:00:11.650
in the brain and body at different rates.

01:00:11.650 --> 01:00:13.340
So it's not instantaneous.

01:00:13.340 --> 01:00:14.900
Some have effects within minutes,

01:00:14.900 --> 01:00:18.350
others within tens of minutes and so on.

01:00:18.350 --> 01:00:19.570
It's really going to depend

01:00:19.570 --> 01:00:21.200
on the pharmacology of those things

01:00:21.200 --> 01:00:22.320
and it's also going to depend

01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:23.760
on whether or not you
have food in your gut,

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:25.200
what else you happen to have circulating

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:26.840
in your blood stream, etc.

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:29.500
But at a very basic level
we can confidently say

01:00:29.500 --> 01:00:32.690
that there are not one, not dozens,

01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:33.730
but as I mentioned before,

01:00:33.730 --> 01:00:35.920
hundreds of studies in
animals and in humans

01:00:35.920 --> 01:00:37.240
that point to the fact that triggering

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:39.990
the increase of adrenaline
late in learning

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:41.330
or immediately after learning

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:42.660
is going to be most beneficial

01:00:42.660 --> 01:00:44.810
if your goal is to retain that information

01:00:44.810 --> 01:00:45.950
for some period of time.

01:00:45.950 --> 01:00:47.630
And to reduce the number of repetitions

01:00:47.630 --> 01:00:49.980
required in order to
learn that information.

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:51.270
Now, I want to acknowledge that

01:00:51.270 --> 01:00:54.090
on previous episodes of this podcast

01:00:54.090 --> 01:00:56.170
and in appearing on other podcasts,

01:00:56.170 --> 01:00:58.980
I've talked a lot about things
like non-sleep deep rest,

01:00:58.980 --> 01:01:01.980
and naps, and sleep as vital
to the learning process.

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:03.010
And I want to emphasize

01:01:03.010 --> 01:01:05.260
that none of that information has changed.

01:01:05.260 --> 01:01:07.360
I don't look at any of that
information differently

01:01:07.360 --> 01:01:09.800
as the consequence of what
I'm talking about today.

01:01:09.800 --> 01:01:12.110
It is still true that the strengthening

01:01:12.110 --> 01:01:13.600
of connections in the brain,

01:01:13.600 --> 01:01:15.500
the literal neural plasticity,

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:17.060
the changing of the circuits occurs

01:01:17.060 --> 01:01:19.730
during deep sleep and non-sleep deep rest.

01:01:19.730 --> 01:01:21.010
And it is also true,

01:01:21.010 --> 01:01:22.870
and I've mentioned these results earlier

01:01:22.870 --> 01:01:25.750
that two papers were
published in Cell Reports,

01:01:25.750 --> 01:01:27.520
Cell Press journal, excellent journal

01:01:27.520 --> 01:01:30.050
over the last few years showing that

01:01:30.050 --> 01:01:34.090
brief naps of about 20 up to 90 minutes

01:01:34.090 --> 01:01:37.470
in some period of time
after an attempt to learn

01:01:37.470 --> 01:01:40.423
can enhance the rate
of learning and memory.

01:01:41.400 --> 01:01:44.760
However, those bouts of sleep,

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:46.360
the deep sleep that night, I should say,

01:01:46.360 --> 01:01:47.950
or those brief naps,

01:01:47.950 --> 01:01:50.520
or even the so-called NSDR as we call it,

01:01:50.520 --> 01:01:52.240
non-sleep deep rest that was used

01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:54.830
to enhance the learning and memory

01:01:54.830 --> 01:01:57.400
of particular pieces of information.

01:01:57.400 --> 01:02:01.000
Either cognitive or physical
information or both.

01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:03.460
That still can be performed,

01:02:03.460 --> 01:02:07.180
but it can be performed some hours later,

01:02:07.180 --> 01:02:08.470
even an hour later.

01:02:08.470 --> 01:02:11.060
It can be performed two hours
later or four hours later.

01:02:11.060 --> 01:02:13.410
Remember, it's in these
naps and in deep sleep

01:02:13.410 --> 01:02:14.930
that the actual reconfiguration

01:02:14.930 --> 01:02:16.120
of the neural circuits occurs,

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:18.980
the strengthening of those
neural circuits occurs.

01:02:18.980 --> 01:02:21.170
It is not the case that you need to finish

01:02:21.170 --> 01:02:23.560
a about of learning and drop
immediately into a nap or sleep.

01:02:23.560 --> 01:02:24.840
Some people might do that,

01:02:24.840 --> 01:02:26.680
but if you're really trying to optimize

01:02:26.680 --> 01:02:28.553
and enhance and improve your memory,

01:02:29.730 --> 01:02:31.090
the data from McGaugh and Cahill

01:02:31.090 --> 01:02:33.700
and many other laboratories
that stemmed out

01:02:33.700 --> 01:02:35.580
from their initial work really point

01:02:35.580 --> 01:02:38.490
to the fact that the
ideal protocol would be

01:02:39.340 --> 01:02:41.660
focus on the thing you're
trying to learn very intensely.

01:02:41.660 --> 01:02:44.270
There are also some other
things like error rates, etc.

01:02:44.270 --> 01:02:45.830
Please see our episodes on learning.

01:02:45.830 --> 01:02:48.360
We have a newsletter
on how to learn better.

01:02:48.360 --> 01:02:49.607
You can access that at HubermanLab.com.

01:02:49.607 --> 01:02:51.760
It's a zero cost newsletter.

01:02:51.760 --> 01:02:53.280
You can grab that PDF.

01:02:53.280 --> 01:02:56.840
It lists out the things to
do during the learning about.

01:02:56.840 --> 01:02:59.320
Still try and get excellent sleep.

01:02:59.320 --> 01:03:01.180
Again, fundamentally
important for mental health,

01:03:01.180 --> 01:03:02.620
physical health, and performance.

01:03:02.620 --> 01:03:04.700
And we can now extend from performance

01:03:04.700 --> 01:03:07.233
to saying including learning and memory.

01:03:08.090 --> 01:03:10.920
Nap if it doesn't interrupt
your nighttime sleep.

01:03:10.920 --> 01:03:13.410
Naps of anywhere from 10 to 90 minutes.

01:03:13.410 --> 01:03:15.010
Or non-sleep deep rest protocols

01:03:15.010 --> 01:03:16.420
will enhance learning and memory,

01:03:16.420 --> 01:03:20.780
but we can now add to that,
that spiking adrenaline

01:03:20.780 --> 01:03:22.840
provided it can be done in a safe way,

01:03:22.840 --> 01:03:25.330
is going to reduce the
number of repetitions

01:03:25.330 --> 01:03:26.163
required to learn.

01:03:26.163 --> 01:03:28.560
And that should be done
at the very tail end

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:30.750
or immediately after a learning about.

01:03:30.750 --> 01:03:32.010
Which is compatible with all

01:03:32.010 --> 01:03:34.170
the other protocols that I mentioned.

01:03:34.170 --> 01:03:35.700
And the reason I'm revisiting this stuff

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:37.570
about sleep and non-sleep deep rest

01:03:37.570 --> 01:03:39.070
is I think that some
people got the impression

01:03:39.070 --> 01:03:41.120
that they need to do that
immediately after learning

01:03:41.120 --> 01:03:42.680
and today I'm saying to the contrary.

01:03:42.680 --> 01:03:44.470
Immediately after learning
you need to go into

01:03:44.470 --> 01:03:47.450
a heightened state of
emotionality and alertness.

01:03:47.450 --> 01:03:49.870
Now it's vitally important to point out

01:03:49.870 --> 01:03:52.600
that you do not need pharmacology.

01:03:52.600 --> 01:03:54.500
You don't need caffeine.

01:03:54.500 --> 01:03:56.710
You don't need Alpha GPC.

01:03:56.710 --> 01:03:58.860
You don't need any pharmacologic substance

01:03:58.860 --> 01:04:01.430
to spike adrenaline
unless that's something

01:04:01.430 --> 01:04:03.340
that you already are doing.

01:04:03.340 --> 01:04:05.050
Or that you can do safely.

01:04:05.050 --> 01:04:06.720
Or that you know you can do safely.

01:04:06.720 --> 01:04:09.210
And I always say, and I'll say it again,

01:04:09.210 --> 01:04:11.160
I'm not a physician so I'm
not prescribing anything.

01:04:11.160 --> 01:04:13.240
I'm a professor, so I profess things.

01:04:13.240 --> 01:04:14.840
You need to what's safe for you.

01:04:14.840 --> 01:04:17.180
So if you're somebody who's
not used to drinking caffeine

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:19.560
and you suddenly drink
four espresso after trying

01:04:19.560 --> 01:04:21.640
to learn something, you are going to have

01:04:21.640 --> 01:04:26.640
a severe increase in alertness
and probably even anxiety.

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:28.300
If you're panic attack prone,

01:04:28.300 --> 01:04:30.290
please don't start taking stimulants

01:04:30.290 --> 01:04:31.760
in order to learn things better.

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:32.593
Please be safe.

01:04:32.593 --> 01:04:33.610
I don't just say that to protect me,

01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:35.053
I say that to protect you.

01:04:36.030 --> 01:04:37.680
And I should mention that if you're

01:04:37.680 --> 01:04:39.550
not accustomed to taking something,

01:04:39.550 --> 01:04:42.230
you always want to first check
with your doctor, of course,

01:04:42.230 --> 01:04:44.750
but also move into that gradually, right?

01:04:44.750 --> 01:04:47.499
Start with the lowest effective dose.

01:04:47.499 --> 01:04:49.320
The minimal effective dose.

01:04:49.320 --> 01:04:51.990
And sometimes the minimal effective dose

01:04:51.990 --> 01:04:54.420
is zero milligrams, it's nothing.

01:04:54.420 --> 01:04:55.253
Why do I say that?

01:04:55.253 --> 01:04:58.090
Well, we already talked
about results where

01:04:58.090 --> 01:05:01.300
they put people's arms into an ice bath

01:05:01.300 --> 01:05:02.920
in order to evoke adrenaline release.

01:05:02.920 --> 01:05:04.330
You are welcome to do that if you want.

01:05:04.330 --> 01:05:07.670
In fact, that's a pretty
low cost, zero pharmacology.

01:05:07.670 --> 01:05:09.320
At least exogenous pharmacology way

01:05:09.320 --> 01:05:11.090
to approach this whole thing.

01:05:11.090 --> 01:05:13.773
That's a way of evoking
your own natural epinephrine

01:05:13.773 --> 01:05:16.510
that turns out also dopamine release.

01:05:16.510 --> 01:05:18.240
You could take a cold shower.

01:05:18.240 --> 01:05:20.005
You could do an ice bath or get into

01:05:20.005 --> 01:05:21.920
a cold circulating bath.

01:05:21.920 --> 01:05:23.650
We've done several episodes

01:05:23.650 --> 01:05:25.830
on the utility of cold for
health and performance.

01:05:25.830 --> 01:05:28.130
You can find those episodes
at HubermanLab.com.

01:05:28.130 --> 01:05:30.830
Also the episode with
my colleague at Stanford

01:05:30.830 --> 01:05:33.090
from the biology department,
Dr. Craig Heller.

01:05:33.090 --> 01:05:35.980
Lots of protocols, in
particular in the episode

01:05:35.980 --> 01:05:37.730
on cold for health and performance.

01:05:37.730 --> 01:05:40.540
That describe how best to use

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:44.650
the cold shower or the ice bath
or the circulating cold bath

01:05:44.650 --> 01:05:46.260
in order to evoke epinephrine

01:05:46.260 --> 01:05:47.333
and dopamine release.

01:05:48.320 --> 01:05:50.640
The point is that the time in which

01:05:50.640 --> 01:05:51.900
you would want to do those protocols

01:05:51.900 --> 01:05:56.310
is after, ideally immediately
after your learning about.

01:05:56.310 --> 01:05:57.840
Meaning when you're sitting down to learn

01:05:57.840 --> 01:05:59.120
new information or after trying

01:05:59.120 --> 01:06:01.320
to learn some new physical skill.

01:06:01.320 --> 01:06:02.510
Now whether or not that's compatible

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:04.750
with the other reasons you're doing

01:06:04.750 --> 01:06:05.900
deliberate cold exposure,

01:06:05.900 --> 01:06:07.310
and whether or not that's compatible

01:06:07.310 --> 01:06:08.360
with the other things you're doing,

01:06:08.360 --> 01:06:10.110
that depends on the
contour of your lifestyle,

01:06:10.110 --> 01:06:11.480
your training, your academic goals,

01:06:11.480 --> 01:06:12.870
your learning goals, etc.

01:06:12.870 --> 01:06:15.640
But if your specific purpose is to enhance

01:06:15.640 --> 01:06:17.727
learning and memory, you want
to spike adrenaline afterwards.

01:06:17.727 --> 01:06:19.340
And so what I'm telling
you is you can do that

01:06:19.340 --> 01:06:20.660
with caffeine.

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:22.810
You can do that with Alpha GPC.

01:06:22.810 --> 01:06:24.240
You can do that with a combination

01:06:24.240 --> 01:06:25.670
of caffeine and Alpha GPC.

01:06:25.670 --> 01:06:27.400
If you can do that safely.

01:06:27.400 --> 01:06:28.480
Some of you I know are using

01:06:28.480 --> 01:06:29.840
other forms of pharmacology.

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:33.043
I did a long episode all about ADHD.

01:06:33.043 --> 01:06:35.720
I have to just really declare
my stance very clearly

01:06:35.720 --> 01:06:39.960
that I am not a fan, I am actually opposed

01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:42.250
to people using prescription drugs

01:06:42.250 --> 01:06:44.540
who are not prescribed those drugs

01:06:44.540 --> 01:06:46.020
in order to enhance alertness.

01:06:46.020 --> 01:06:47.760
I think there's a big addictive potential.

01:06:47.760 --> 01:06:49.660
There also is a potential to really

01:06:49.660 --> 01:06:51.530
disrupt one's own pharmacology around

01:06:51.530 --> 01:06:53.850
the dopaminergic system.

01:06:53.850 --> 01:06:56.660
However, some of you I know are prescribed

01:06:56.660 --> 01:07:00.270
things like ritalin,
Adderall and modafinil

01:07:00.270 --> 01:07:01.340
and things of that sort in order

01:07:01.340 --> 01:07:03.020
to increase alertness and focus.

01:07:03.020 --> 01:07:04.360
So for those of you that are prescribed

01:07:04.360 --> 01:07:07.700
those things from a board
certified physician,

01:07:07.700 --> 01:07:08.660
you're going to have to decide

01:07:08.660 --> 01:07:10.310
if you're going to take
them before trying to learn

01:07:10.310 --> 01:07:12.060
or after trying to learn.

01:07:12.060 --> 01:07:13.420
You also have to take into consideration

01:07:13.420 --> 01:07:15.470
that some of those drugs
are very long acting.

01:07:15.470 --> 01:07:17.130
Some are shorter acting.

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:18.400
And time that according

01:07:18.400 --> 01:07:20.230
to what you're trying to learn and when.

01:07:20.230 --> 01:07:21.830
So that's pharmacology.

01:07:21.830 --> 01:07:24.830
But as I mentioned, there
are the behavioral protocols.

01:07:24.830 --> 01:07:28.840
You can use cold and cold
is an excellent stimulus

01:07:28.840 --> 01:07:32.900
because first of all, it
doesn't involve pharmacology.

01:07:32.900 --> 01:07:35.710
Second of all, you can generally access it

01:07:35.710 --> 01:07:39.380
at low to zero cost, especially
the cold shower approach.

01:07:39.380 --> 01:07:42.000
And third, you can titrate it.

01:07:42.000 --> 01:07:43.730
You can start with warmer water.

01:07:43.730 --> 01:07:46.010
You can make it very, very
cold if that's your thing

01:07:46.010 --> 01:07:47.540
and you're able to tolerate that safely.

01:07:47.540 --> 01:07:50.830
You can make it moderately cold.

01:07:50.830 --> 01:07:53.610
How cold should it be in order
to invoke adrenaline release?

01:07:53.610 --> 01:07:55.540
Well, it should be uncomfortably cold

01:07:55.540 --> 01:07:58.010
but cold enough that you feel like

01:07:58.010 --> 01:07:59.010
you really want to get out,

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:00.370
but can stay in safely.

01:08:00.370 --> 01:08:02.590
That's going to evoke adrenaline release.

01:08:02.590 --> 01:08:04.160
If it quickens your breathing,

01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:06.090
if it makes you go wide eyed.

01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:07.570
That's increasing adrenaline release.

01:08:07.570 --> 01:08:09.850
In fact, those effects of going wide eyed

01:08:09.850 --> 01:08:10.830
and quickening of the breathing

01:08:10.830 --> 01:08:12.750
and the challenges in thinking clearly,

01:08:12.750 --> 01:08:14.400
those are the direct effects of adrenaline

01:08:14.400 --> 01:08:15.750
on your brain and body.

01:08:15.750 --> 01:08:16.960
And of course, there are other ways

01:08:16.960 --> 01:08:18.190
to increase adrenaline.

01:08:18.190 --> 01:08:20.760
You could go out for a hard run.

01:08:20.760 --> 01:08:22.850
You could do any number of things

01:08:22.850 --> 01:08:25.120
that would increase
adrenaline in your body.

01:08:25.120 --> 01:08:27.470
Which things you choose is up to you,

01:08:27.470 --> 01:08:30.850
but from a very clear, solid grounding

01:08:30.850 --> 01:08:33.700
in research data, we can confidently say

01:08:33.700 --> 01:08:36.700
that spiking adrenaline after interacting

01:08:36.700 --> 01:08:38.950
with some material, physical
or cognitive material

01:08:38.950 --> 01:08:39.880
that you're trying to learn,

01:08:39.880 --> 01:08:42.420
is going to be the best time
to spike that adrenaline.

01:08:42.420 --> 01:08:44.280
Now I realize I'm being
a bit redundant today

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:46.220
or perhaps a lot redundant.

01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:48.770
In repeating over and over that

01:08:48.770 --> 01:08:51.440
the increase in epinephrine should occur

01:08:51.440 --> 01:08:54.320
either very late in an
attempt to learn something

01:08:54.320 --> 01:08:58.220
or immediately after an
attempt to learn something.

01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:00.980
I also want to emphasize
the general contour

01:09:00.980 --> 01:09:03.670
of pharmacologic effects
and of behavioral tools

01:09:03.670 --> 01:09:04.930
to create adrenaline.

01:09:04.930 --> 01:09:06.300
What do I mean by that sentence?

01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:09.300
What I mean is that McGaugh and colleagues

01:09:09.300 --> 01:09:10.470
explored a huge number

01:09:10.470 --> 01:09:11.770
of different compounds and approaches.

01:09:11.770 --> 01:09:13.810
Everything from the hand into the ice bath

01:09:13.810 --> 01:09:17.430
to injecting adrenaline, to caffeine,

01:09:17.430 --> 01:09:22.360
to drugs that block the affects
of adrenaline and caffeine.

01:09:22.360 --> 01:09:24.370
Drugs like muscimol and picrotoxin.

01:09:24.370 --> 01:09:25.203
Please don't take those.

01:09:25.203 --> 01:09:27.040
These are drugs that reduce

01:09:27.040 --> 01:09:28.600
or enhance the amount of adrenaline

01:09:28.600 --> 01:09:31.680
and the overall takeaway is that anything

01:09:31.680 --> 01:09:34.540
that increases adrenaline will increase

01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:35.950
learning and memory and will reduce

01:09:35.950 --> 01:09:39.200
the number of repetitions
required to learn something.

01:09:39.200 --> 01:09:40.910
Regardless of whether
or not that something

01:09:40.910 --> 01:09:44.820
has an emotional intensity or not.

01:09:44.820 --> 01:09:48.110
Provided that spike in
adrenaline occurs late

01:09:48.110 --> 01:09:49.630
in the learning or immediately after.

01:09:49.630 --> 01:09:53.760
And anything that reduces
epinephrine and adrenaline

01:09:53.760 --> 01:09:55.780
will impair learning.

01:09:55.780 --> 01:09:58.590
And that's the key and
novel piece of information

01:09:58.590 --> 01:09:59.630
that I'm adding now.

01:09:59.630 --> 01:10:03.100
Which is if you're taking
beta blockers, for instance.

01:10:03.100 --> 01:10:05.590
Or if you're trying to learn something

01:10:05.590 --> 01:10:09.010
and it's not evoking much
of an emotional response,

01:10:09.010 --> 01:10:10.790
and you're not using any pharmacology

01:10:10.790 --> 01:10:13.670
or other methods to
enhance adrenaline release

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:15.310
after learning that thing, well,

01:10:15.310 --> 01:10:17.130
you're not going to learn it very well.

01:10:17.130 --> 01:10:20.600
In fact, McGaugh and Cahill
did beautiful experiments

01:10:20.600 --> 01:10:23.900
in humans looking at how much adrenaline

01:10:23.900 --> 01:10:28.670
is increased by varying
the emotional intensity

01:10:28.670 --> 01:10:29.870
of different things that they were trying

01:10:29.870 --> 01:10:31.210
to get people to learn.

01:10:31.210 --> 01:10:34.028
Or by changing the dosage of epinephrine.

01:10:34.028 --> 01:10:36.540
Or by changing the amount
of epinephrine blocker

01:10:36.540 --> 01:10:37.390
that they injected.

01:10:37.390 --> 01:10:39.460
Lots and lots of studies.

01:10:39.460 --> 01:10:41.200
The key thing to take
away from those studies

01:10:41.200 --> 01:10:43.270
is that for some people, adrenaline

01:10:43.270 --> 01:10:46.320
was increased 600 to 700%.

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:48.940
So six to seven fold over baseline

01:10:48.940 --> 01:10:51.740
in the amount of circulating
epinephrine or adrenaline.

01:10:51.740 --> 01:10:53.430
And keep in mind, sometimes that increase

01:10:53.430 --> 01:10:55.540
was due to the actual thing
they were trying to learn

01:10:55.540 --> 01:10:58.660
being very emotional,
positive or negative emotion.

01:10:58.660 --> 01:11:00.310
And sometimes it was
because they were using

01:11:00.310 --> 01:11:02.950
a pharmacologic approach
or the ice bath approach.

01:11:02.950 --> 01:11:04.610
I don't think they ever
used a cold shower approach,

01:11:04.610 --> 01:11:06.240
but that would have been
a very effective one

01:11:06.240 --> 01:11:07.420
we can be sure.

01:11:07.420 --> 01:11:12.420
However, other people had
a zero to 10% increase.

01:11:13.040 --> 01:11:15.193
So a very small increase in epinephrine.

01:11:16.270 --> 01:11:18.100
What we can confidently say on the basis

01:11:18.100 --> 01:11:22.173
of all those data is that
the more epinephrine release,

01:11:22.173 --> 01:11:25.860
the better that people
remembered the material.

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:27.240
Over and over again this was shown.

01:11:27.240 --> 01:11:29.090
Whether or not it was
for cognitive material,

01:11:29.090 --> 01:11:31.790
so learning a language,
learning a passage of words,

01:11:31.790 --> 01:11:33.170
learning mathematics.

01:11:33.170 --> 01:11:35.820
Or whether or not it was
for physical learning.

01:11:35.820 --> 01:11:37.730
I want to emphasize something
about physical learning

01:11:37.730 --> 01:11:40.050
because I know a number
of you are probably

01:11:40.050 --> 01:11:41.600
drinking a cup of coffee or having

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:43.950
a cup of yerba mate or
maybe even an energy drink

01:11:43.950 --> 01:11:45.680
and taking some Alpha GPC or something

01:11:45.680 --> 01:11:48.350
before physical exercise.

01:11:48.350 --> 01:11:49.860
I'm not saying that's a bad thing to do

01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:51.050
or you wouldn't want to do that.

01:11:51.050 --> 01:11:53.400
But that's really to increase alertness.

01:11:53.400 --> 01:11:54.700
It won't enhance learning,

01:11:54.700 --> 01:11:57.790
at least not as well as
doing those things after

01:11:57.790 --> 01:11:59.110
the physical exercise.

01:11:59.110 --> 01:12:01.650
Now again, many of you, including myself,

01:12:01.650 --> 01:12:03.630
exercise for the sake
of the physical benefits

01:12:03.630 --> 01:12:04.463
of that exercise.

01:12:04.463 --> 01:12:06.540
So cardiovascular, resistance training.

01:12:06.540 --> 01:12:09.900
But we're not really focused
on learning and memory.

01:12:09.900 --> 01:12:13.200
So, I emphasize this just so it's

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:15.285
immensely clear to everybody.

01:12:15.285 --> 01:12:16.840
If you want to use those approaches

01:12:16.840 --> 01:12:18.990
of increasing adrenaline prior to

01:12:18.990 --> 01:12:20.860
or during physical training,

01:12:20.860 --> 01:12:23.590
or cognitive work for
that matter, be my guest.

01:12:23.590 --> 01:12:24.590
I think that's perfectly fine,

01:12:24.590 --> 01:12:26.330
provided that's safe for you.

01:12:26.330 --> 01:12:28.850
It's only by moving it to late

01:12:28.850 --> 01:12:31.420
or after the learning that
you're really shifting

01:12:31.420 --> 01:12:34.100
the role of that adrenaline increase

01:12:34.100 --> 01:12:36.590
to enhancing memory specifically.

01:12:36.590 --> 01:12:38.990
And as a cautionary note, don't think

01:12:38.990 --> 01:12:40.900
that you can push this entire system

01:12:40.900 --> 01:12:42.770
to the extreme over and over again,

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:45.450
or chronically, as we
say, and get away with it.

01:12:45.450 --> 01:12:47.950
In other words, you're
not going to be able

01:12:47.950 --> 01:12:51.795
to take a Alpha GPC and a double espresso

01:12:51.795 --> 01:12:56.170
do your focus about of work,
cognitive or physical work,

01:12:56.170 --> 01:12:58.830
and then spike adrenaline again afterwards

01:12:58.830 --> 01:13:00.610
and remember that stuff
you did better, right.

01:13:00.610 --> 01:13:03.680
I'm not encouraging you, in
fact I'm discouraging you

01:13:03.680 --> 01:13:07.010
from chronically increasing adrenaline

01:13:07.010 --> 01:13:11.890
both during and after
a given about of work

01:13:11.890 --> 01:13:13.480
if the goal is to learn.

01:13:13.480 --> 01:13:14.420
Why do I say that?

01:13:14.420 --> 01:13:17.630
Well, work from McGaugh
and Cahill and others

01:13:17.630 --> 01:13:21.430
has shown that it's
not the absolute amount

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:23.340
of adrenaline that you release

01:13:23.340 --> 01:13:26.600
in your brain and body that
matters for enhancing memory.

01:13:26.600 --> 01:13:29.730
It's the amount of adrenaline you release

01:13:29.730 --> 01:13:32.270
relative to the amount of adrenaline

01:13:32.270 --> 01:13:34.681
that was in your system just prior.

01:13:34.681 --> 01:13:36.370
Particularly in the hour or two prior.

01:13:36.370 --> 01:13:37.890
So again, it's the delta, as we say.

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:38.970
It's the difference.

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:41.230
So if you're going to
chronically increase adrenaline

01:13:41.230 --> 01:13:43.200
you're not going to learn as well.

01:13:43.200 --> 01:13:45.890
The real key is to have
adrenaline modestly low.

01:13:45.890 --> 01:13:48.160
Perhaps even just as much as you need

01:13:48.160 --> 01:13:50.040
in order to be able to focus on something,

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:52.900
pay attention to it, and
then spike it afterwards.

01:13:52.900 --> 01:13:55.920
This is immensely important because

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:58.260
while much of what we're talking about

01:13:58.260 --> 01:13:59.880
is actually a form of inducing

01:13:59.880 --> 01:14:02.020
a neuro chemical acute stress.

01:14:02.020 --> 01:14:06.330
Meaning a brief and rapid onset of stress.

01:14:06.330 --> 01:14:10.010
Well, chronic stress,
the chronic elevation

01:14:10.010 --> 01:14:11.900
of epinephrine and cortisol

01:14:11.900 --> 01:14:13.660
is actually detrimental to learning.

01:14:13.660 --> 01:14:16.540
And there's an entire
category of literature

01:14:16.540 --> 01:14:18.440
mainly from the work of the great

01:14:18.440 --> 01:14:20.700
and sadly the late Bruce McEwen

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:22.040
from the Rockefeller University.

01:14:22.040 --> 01:14:23.600
And some of his scientific offspring

01:14:23.600 --> 01:14:25.700
like the great Robert Sapolsky,

01:14:25.700 --> 01:14:27.810
showing that chronic stress,

01:14:27.810 --> 01:14:29.380
chronic elevation of epinephrine

01:14:29.380 --> 01:14:31.500
actually inhibits learning and memory.

01:14:31.500 --> 01:14:33.420
And also can inhibit
immune system function.

01:14:33.420 --> 01:14:36.630
Whereas acute, right, sharp increases

01:14:36.630 --> 01:14:40.030
in adrenaline and cortisol
actually can enhance learning

01:14:40.030 --> 01:14:41.960
and indeed, can enhance the immune system.

01:14:41.960 --> 01:14:44.820
So if you really want to
leverage this information,

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:47.610
you might consider getting
your brain and body

01:14:47.610 --> 01:14:50.820
into a very calm and yet alert state.

01:14:50.820 --> 01:14:52.210
So a high attentional state

01:14:52.210 --> 01:14:54.070
that will allow you to focus on what it is

01:14:54.070 --> 01:14:55.240
that you're trying to learn.

01:14:55.240 --> 01:14:58.350
We know focus is vital
for encoding information

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:00.310
and for triggering neuroplasticity.

01:15:00.310 --> 01:15:03.020
But remaining calm throughout that time

01:15:03.020 --> 01:15:05.900
and then afterwards spiking adrenaline

01:15:05.900 --> 01:15:08.780
and allowing adrenaline to
have these incredible effects

01:15:08.780 --> 01:15:10.500
on reducing the number of repetitions

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:11.660
required to learn.

01:15:11.660 --> 01:15:14.230
So if you're like me, you're
learning about this information

01:15:14.230 --> 01:15:16.690
this beautiful work of
McGaugh and Cahill and others

01:15:16.690 --> 01:15:19.770
and thinking, wow, I
should perhaps consider

01:15:19.770 --> 01:15:23.100
spiking my adrenaline
in one form or another

01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:25.360
at the tail end or immediately following

01:15:25.360 --> 01:15:27.260
an attempt to learn something.

01:15:27.260 --> 01:15:29.040
And yet, we are not the first

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:30.070
to have this conversation.

01:15:30.070 --> 01:15:31.570
Nor were McGaugh and Cahill

01:15:31.570 --> 01:15:34.080
or any other researchers
that I've discussed today

01:15:35.190 --> 01:15:37.810
the first to start using this technique.

01:15:37.810 --> 01:15:40.170
In fact, there is a beautiful review

01:15:40.170 --> 01:15:42.790
that was published just
this year, May of 2022

01:15:42.790 --> 01:15:44.480
in the journal Neuron, Cell Press Journal.

01:15:44.480 --> 01:15:46.010
Excellent journal.

01:15:46.010 --> 01:15:49.010
Called Mechanisms of Memory Under Stress.

01:15:49.010 --> 01:15:52.170
And I just want to read to you
the first opening paragraph

01:15:52.170 --> 01:15:54.430
of this review, which
is, as the name suggests,

01:15:54.430 --> 01:15:56.810
all about memory and stress.

01:15:56.810 --> 01:15:58.717
So here I'm reading, and I quote,

01:15:58.717 --> 01:16:00.837
"In Medieval times communities threw"

01:16:00.837 --> 01:16:02.217
"young children in the river when"

01:16:02.217 --> 01:16:04.817
"they wanted them to
remember important events."

01:16:04.817 --> 01:16:06.647
"They believed that throwing
a child in the water"

01:16:06.647 --> 01:16:08.927
"after witnessing historic proceedings"

01:16:08.927 --> 01:16:10.277
"would leave a life long memory"

01:16:10.277 --> 01:16:12.570
"for the events in the child."

01:16:12.570 --> 01:16:14.440
Believe it or not, this is true.

01:16:14.440 --> 01:16:19.023
This is a practice that
somehow people arrived at.

01:16:19.930 --> 01:16:22.240
I don't know if they were
aware of what adrenaline was.

01:16:22.240 --> 01:16:23.410
Probably not.

01:16:23.410 --> 01:16:26.090
But somehow in medieval times

01:16:26.090 --> 01:16:28.910
it was understood that spiking adrenaline

01:16:28.910 --> 01:16:32.210
or creating a robust emotional experience

01:16:32.210 --> 01:16:36.740
after an experience that one
hoped a child would learn

01:16:36.740 --> 01:16:39.385
would encourage the
child's nervous system,

01:16:39.385 --> 01:16:40.460
they didn't even know
what a nervous system was,

01:16:40.460 --> 01:16:42.420
but would encourage the brain and body

01:16:42.420 --> 01:16:45.913
of that child to remember
those particular events.

01:16:46.860 --> 01:16:48.930
Very counter intuitive if you ask me.

01:16:48.930 --> 01:16:50.700
I would have thought that
the kid would remember

01:16:50.700 --> 01:16:52.140
only being thrown into the river.

01:16:52.140 --> 01:16:53.560
My guess is that they remember that,

01:16:53.560 --> 01:16:56.530
but the idea here anyway,
is they also remember

01:16:56.530 --> 01:16:59.170
the things that preceded
being thrown into the river.

01:16:59.170 --> 01:17:02.590
So both interesting and amusing

01:17:02.590 --> 01:17:06.670
and somewhat, I should say
thought stimulating, really.

01:17:06.670 --> 01:17:09.670
That this is a practice
that has been going on

01:17:09.670 --> 01:17:11.720
for many hundreds of years.

01:17:11.720 --> 01:17:14.180
And we are not the first to start thinking

01:17:14.180 --> 01:17:16.830
about using cold water as
an adrenaline stimulus.

01:17:16.830 --> 01:17:18.360
Nor are we the first
to start thinking about

01:17:18.360 --> 01:17:20.810
using cold water induced adrenaline

01:17:20.810 --> 01:17:22.620
as a way to enhance learning and memory.

01:17:22.620 --> 01:17:25.360
This has been happening
since medieval times.

01:17:25.360 --> 01:17:27.240
So up until now I've been talking about

01:17:27.240 --> 01:17:29.930
pretty broad contour of these experiments.

01:17:29.930 --> 01:17:32.000
I've been talking about the
underlying pharmacology,

01:17:32.000 --> 01:17:33.360
the role of epinephrine and so forth.

01:17:33.360 --> 01:17:34.770
I haven't really talked a lot about

01:17:34.770 --> 01:17:36.300
the underlying neural mechanisms.

01:17:36.300 --> 01:17:38.125
So we're just going to
take a minute or two

01:17:38.125 --> 01:17:39.234
and describe those for you

01:17:39.234 --> 01:17:40.840
because they are informative.

01:17:40.840 --> 01:17:43.570
We all have a brain structure
called the amygdala.

01:17:43.570 --> 01:17:45.610
A lot of people think
it's associated with fear

01:17:45.610 --> 01:17:48.860
but it's actually associated
with threat detection

01:17:48.860 --> 01:17:52.170
and more generally, and I
should say more specifically,

01:17:52.170 --> 01:17:54.980
with detecting what sorts of events

01:17:54.980 --> 01:17:57.650
in the environment are
novel and are linked

01:17:57.650 --> 01:17:59.530
to particular emotional states.

01:17:59.530 --> 01:18:01.090
Both positive emotional states

01:18:01.090 --> 01:18:03.070
and negative emotional states.

01:18:03.070 --> 01:18:06.160
So the neurons in the
amygdala are exquisitely good

01:18:06.160 --> 01:18:09.320
at figuring out, right, they
don't have their own mind

01:18:09.320 --> 01:18:12.250
but at detecting correlations between

01:18:12.250 --> 01:18:13.820
sensory events in the environment

01:18:13.820 --> 01:18:15.660
that trigger the release of adrenaline

01:18:15.660 --> 01:18:17.250
and what's going on in the brain.

01:18:17.250 --> 01:18:19.300
And because the amygdala is so extensively

01:18:19.300 --> 01:18:21.310
interconnected with
other areas of the brain.

01:18:21.310 --> 01:18:22.470
It basically connects to everything

01:18:22.470 --> 01:18:24.220
and everything connects back to it.

01:18:25.110 --> 01:18:27.990
The amygdala is in a
position to strengthen

01:18:27.990 --> 01:18:32.970
particular connections
in the brain very easily.

01:18:32.970 --> 01:18:34.420
Provided certain conditions are met.

01:18:34.420 --> 01:18:35.400
And those conditions are the ones

01:18:35.400 --> 01:18:37.460
we've been talking about up until now.

01:18:37.460 --> 01:18:39.640
Emotional saliency that
results in increases

01:18:39.640 --> 01:18:41.450
in epinephrine and cortisol.

01:18:41.450 --> 01:18:43.720
Or circulating epinephrine and cortisol

01:18:43.720 --> 01:18:45.630
being much higher than it was 10 minutes

01:18:45.630 --> 01:18:46.820
or 15 minutes before.

01:18:46.820 --> 01:18:48.490
And the net effect of the amygdala

01:18:48.490 --> 01:18:51.190
in this context is to
take whatever patterns

01:18:51.190 --> 01:18:53.370
of neural activity preceded that increase

01:18:53.370 --> 01:18:55.250
in adrenaline and corticosterone

01:18:55.250 --> 01:18:57.930
and strengthen those
synapses that were involved

01:18:57.930 --> 01:18:59.240
in that neural activity.

01:18:59.240 --> 01:19:01.170
So the amygdala doesn't have knowledge.

01:19:01.170 --> 01:19:03.070
It's not a thinking area.

01:19:03.070 --> 01:19:05.430
It's a correlation detector.

01:19:05.430 --> 01:19:06.900
And it's correlating neural chemical

01:19:06.900 --> 01:19:08.240
states of the brain and body,

01:19:08.240 --> 01:19:11.470
different patterns of electrical
activity in the brain.

01:19:11.470 --> 01:19:14.840
This is important because
it really emphasizes

01:19:14.840 --> 01:19:19.370
the fact that both negative
and positive emotional states

01:19:19.370 --> 01:19:23.580
and the different but somewhat overlapping

01:19:23.580 --> 01:19:25.490
chemical states that they create,

01:19:25.490 --> 01:19:28.670
or the conditions, as we say the and gates

01:19:28.670 --> 01:19:30.790
through which memory is laid down.

01:19:30.790 --> 01:19:32.300
And gates will be familiar to those

01:19:32.300 --> 01:19:35.000
of you who have done a bit
of a computer programming.

01:19:35.000 --> 01:19:37.410
An and gate is simply a condition

01:19:37.410 --> 01:19:42.100
in which you need one
thing and another to happen

01:19:42.100 --> 01:19:44.440
in order for a third thing to happen.

01:19:44.440 --> 01:19:47.240
So you need epinephrine
elevated and you need

01:19:47.240 --> 01:19:49.290
robust activity in a
particular brain circuit

01:19:49.290 --> 01:19:52.570
if in fact that brain circuit
is going to be strengthened.

01:19:52.570 --> 01:19:55.760
It's not sufficient to have one
or the other, you need both.

01:19:55.760 --> 01:19:57.280
Hence, the name and gate.

01:19:57.280 --> 01:19:59.430
And the amygdala is very
good at establishing

01:19:59.430 --> 01:20:01.510
these and gate contingencies.

01:20:01.510 --> 01:20:03.850
It's also a very generic brain structure

01:20:03.850 --> 01:20:06.420
in the sense that it doesn't really care

01:20:06.420 --> 01:20:09.270
what sorts of sensory events are involved

01:20:09.270 --> 01:20:11.140
provided they correlated in time

01:20:11.140 --> 01:20:14.190
with that increase in
adrenal and corticosterone.

01:20:14.190 --> 01:20:17.840
This has a wonderful side
and a kind of dark side.

01:20:17.840 --> 01:20:22.330
The dark side is that PTSD and traumas

01:20:22.330 --> 01:20:26.010
of various kinds often involve an increase

01:20:26.010 --> 01:20:28.700
in adrenaline because whatever it was

01:20:28.700 --> 01:20:31.090
that caused the PTSD was
indeed very stressful.

01:20:31.090 --> 01:20:33.180
Caused these big increases
in these chemicals.

01:20:33.180 --> 01:20:37.700
And because the amygdala is
rather general in its functions.

01:20:37.700 --> 01:20:39.810
Right, it's not tuned or designed

01:20:39.810 --> 01:20:42.310
in any kind of way to
be specifically active

01:20:42.310 --> 01:20:44.400
in response to particular types

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:47.100
of sensory events, or perceptions.

01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:48.600
Well, then what it means is that

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:50.560
we can start to become afraid

01:20:50.560 --> 01:20:55.200
of entire city blocks where
one bad thing happened

01:20:55.200 --> 01:20:57.170
in a particular room of
a particular building

01:20:57.170 --> 01:20:58.270
in a city block.

01:20:58.270 --> 01:21:01.700
We can become fearful of
anyplace that contains

01:21:01.700 --> 01:21:04.300
a lot of people if
something bad happened to us

01:21:04.300 --> 01:21:06.460
in a place that contained a lot of people.

01:21:06.460 --> 01:21:09.400
The amygdala is not so much of a splitter,

01:21:09.400 --> 01:21:10.430
as we say in science.

01:21:10.430 --> 01:21:11.870
We talk about lumpers and splitters.

01:21:11.870 --> 01:21:13.520
Lumpers are kind of generalizers,

01:21:15.300 --> 01:21:16.420
if that's even a word.

01:21:16.420 --> 01:21:18.189
And I think it is, someone will tell me

01:21:18.189 --> 01:21:19.615
one way or the other.

01:21:19.615 --> 01:21:23.240
And splitters are people
that are ultra precise

01:21:23.240 --> 01:21:26.520
and specific and nuanced
about every little detail.

01:21:26.520 --> 01:21:29.600
The amygdala is more of
a lumper than a splitter

01:21:29.600 --> 01:21:31.600
when it comes to sensory events.

01:21:31.600 --> 01:21:33.690
Other areas of the
brain only become active

01:21:33.690 --> 01:21:35.630
under very, very specific conditions

01:21:35.630 --> 01:21:36.830
and only those conditions.

01:21:36.830 --> 01:21:40.790
And similarly, epinephrine
is just a molecule.

01:21:40.790 --> 01:21:43.540
It's just a chemical that's circulating

01:21:43.540 --> 01:21:44.600
in our brain and body.

01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:47.090
There's no epinephrine specifically for

01:21:47.090 --> 01:21:50.120
a cold shower that is
distinct from the epinephrine

01:21:50.120 --> 01:21:51.250
associated with a bad event

01:21:51.250 --> 01:21:52.810
which is distinct from the epinephrine

01:21:52.810 --> 01:21:54.400
associated with a really exciting event

01:21:54.400 --> 01:21:55.380
that makes you really alert.

01:21:55.380 --> 01:21:57.750
Epinephrine is just a
molecule, it's generic.

01:21:57.750 --> 01:21:59.800
So these systems have a lot of overlap

01:21:59.800 --> 01:22:02.380
and that can explain, in large part,

01:22:02.380 --> 01:22:05.610
why when good things happen
in particular locations

01:22:05.610 --> 01:22:08.320
and in the company of particular people,

01:22:08.320 --> 01:22:11.650
we often generalize to large categories

01:22:11.650 --> 01:22:13.460
of people, places, and things.

01:22:13.460 --> 01:22:15.080
And when negative things happen

01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:16.180
in particular circumstances,

01:22:16.180 --> 01:22:19.140
we often generalize about
people places and things

01:22:19.140 --> 01:22:20.910
associated with that negative event.

01:22:20.910 --> 01:22:22.690
So now I'd like to talk about other tools

01:22:22.690 --> 01:22:24.520
that you can leverage that have been shown

01:22:24.520 --> 01:22:26.040
in quality, peer-reviewed studies

01:22:26.040 --> 01:22:27.980
to enhance learning and memory.

01:22:27.980 --> 01:22:29.740
And perhaps one of the most potent

01:22:29.740 --> 01:22:32.684
of those tools is exercise.

01:22:32.684 --> 01:22:35.350
There are numerous studies on this

01:22:35.350 --> 01:22:39.440
in both animals and
fortunately now also in humans.

01:22:39.440 --> 01:22:42.260
Thanks to the beautiful work
of people like Wendy Suzuki

01:22:42.260 --> 01:22:43.780
from New York University.

01:22:43.780 --> 01:22:47.090
Wendy's lab has identified
how exercise works

01:22:47.090 --> 01:22:48.630
to enhance learning and memory

01:22:48.630 --> 01:22:51.280
and other forms of
cognition, I should mention.

01:22:51.280 --> 01:22:54.520
As well as things that can augment,

01:22:54.520 --> 01:22:56.850
can enhance the effects of exercise

01:22:56.850 --> 01:22:59.870
on learning and memory and
other forms of cognition.

01:22:59.870 --> 01:23:01.840
Wendy is going to be a
guest on this podcast.

01:23:01.840 --> 01:23:05.020
It's actually the episode
that follows this episode.

01:23:05.020 --> 01:23:06.370
And it includes a lot of material

01:23:06.370 --> 01:23:08.150
that we have not covered today.

01:23:08.150 --> 01:23:10.760
And she's an incredible scientist

01:23:10.760 --> 01:23:12.630
and has some incredible findings

01:23:12.630 --> 01:23:15.570
that I know everyone is going
to find immensely useful.

01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:17.770
In the meantime, I want to talk about some

01:23:17.770 --> 01:23:20.017
of the general effects
of exercise on learning

01:23:20.017 --> 01:23:22.030
and memory that she's discovered

01:23:22.030 --> 01:23:24.310
and that other laboratories
have discovered.

01:23:24.310 --> 01:23:25.690
If you recall earlier, I mentioned

01:23:25.690 --> 01:23:29.150
that learning and memory
almost always involves

01:23:29.150 --> 01:23:32.080
the strengthening of particular synapses

01:23:32.080 --> 01:23:34.000
and neural circuits in the brain.

01:23:34.000 --> 01:23:37.280
And not so much the increase in the number

01:23:37.280 --> 01:23:39.210
of neurons in the brain.

01:23:39.210 --> 01:23:41.010
There is one exception, however.

01:23:41.010 --> 01:23:43.680
And we now have both animal
data and some human data

01:23:43.680 --> 01:23:46.540
to support the fact that
cardiovascular exercise

01:23:46.540 --> 01:23:51.230
seems to increase what we call
dentate gyrus neurogenesis.

01:23:51.230 --> 01:23:53.500
Neurogenesis is the
creation of new neurons.

01:23:53.500 --> 01:23:56.260
The dentate gyrus is a
subregion of the hippocampus

01:23:56.260 --> 01:23:58.260
that's involved in learning and memory

01:23:58.260 --> 01:23:59.610
of particular kinds.

01:23:59.610 --> 01:24:01.630
Right, certain types of events,

01:24:01.630 --> 01:24:02.960
particular contextual learning,

01:24:02.960 --> 01:24:04.900
but some other things as well.

01:24:04.900 --> 01:24:06.700
Sometimes involved in spacial learning.

01:24:06.700 --> 01:24:08.290
There's a lot debate about exactly what

01:24:08.290 --> 01:24:10.180
the dentate gyrus does, but for the sake

01:24:10.180 --> 01:24:13.710
of this discussion, and I think everyone

01:24:13.710 --> 01:24:15.030
in the neuroscience community would agree

01:24:15.030 --> 01:24:16.660
that the dentate gyrus is important

01:24:16.660 --> 01:24:19.053
for memory formation and consolidation.

01:24:21.070 --> 01:24:24.300
The dentate gyrus does seem
to be one region in the brain,

01:24:24.300 --> 01:24:25.580
certainly in the rodent brain,

01:24:25.580 --> 01:24:29.130
but more and more it's seeming
also in the human brain

01:24:29.130 --> 01:24:31.310
where at least some new neurons

01:24:31.310 --> 01:24:33.210
are added throughout the lifespan.

01:24:33.210 --> 01:24:36.750
And, as it turns out, that
cardiovascular exercise

01:24:36.750 --> 01:24:38.880
can increase the proliferation

01:24:38.880 --> 01:24:40.930
of new neurons in this structure.

01:24:40.930 --> 01:24:44.080
And that those new neurons, excuse me,

01:24:44.080 --> 01:24:46.580
are important for the formation

01:24:46.580 --> 01:24:48.630
of certain types of new memories.

01:24:48.630 --> 01:24:50.490
There are wonderful data showing that

01:24:50.490 --> 01:24:52.780
if you use X-irradiation, which is a way

01:24:52.780 --> 01:24:55.250
to eliminate the formation
of those new cells

01:24:55.250 --> 01:24:57.820
or other tools and tricks to eliminate

01:24:57.820 --> 01:24:59.180
the formation of those cells

01:24:59.180 --> 01:25:01.590
that you block the
formation of certain kinds

01:25:01.590 --> 01:25:02.520
of learning and memory.

01:25:02.520 --> 01:25:03.353
What does this mean?

01:25:03.353 --> 01:25:05.660
Well, there are a lot of
reasons for the statement

01:25:05.660 --> 01:25:08.610
I'm about to make that extend far beyond

01:25:08.610 --> 01:25:11.060
neurogenesis and the
hippocampus learning and memory.

01:25:11.060 --> 01:25:12.920
But it's very clear that getting anywhere

01:25:12.920 --> 01:25:15.620
from 180, or I should say a minimum

01:25:15.620 --> 01:25:18.810
of 180 to 200 minutes
of so called zone two

01:25:18.810 --> 01:25:21.820
cardiovascular exercise, so
this is cardiovascular exercise

01:25:21.820 --> 01:25:25.520
that can be performed
at a pretty steady state

01:25:25.520 --> 01:25:28.140
which would allow you to just
barely hold a conversation.

01:25:28.140 --> 01:25:31.050
So breathing hard but not super hard.

01:25:31.050 --> 01:25:33.360
Such as in sprints or high
intensity interval training.

01:25:33.360 --> 01:25:37.050
But doing that for 180 to
200 minutes per week total

01:25:37.050 --> 01:25:39.700
is it appears the minimum threshold

01:25:39.700 --> 01:25:43.190
for enhancing some of
the longevity effects

01:25:43.190 --> 01:25:46.300
associated with improvements
in cardiovascular fitness

01:25:46.300 --> 01:25:49.010
and we believe that it is indirectly,

01:25:49.010 --> 01:25:51.320
I should say indirectly,
through enhancements

01:25:51.320 --> 01:25:54.180
in cardiovascular fitness
that there are improvements

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:56.950
in hippocampal dentate gyrus neurogenesis.

01:25:56.950 --> 01:25:57.990
What does that mean?

01:25:57.990 --> 01:26:00.600
The improvements in
cardiovascular function

01:26:00.600 --> 01:26:02.740
are indirectly impacting the ability

01:26:02.740 --> 01:26:05.200
of the dentate gyrus to
create these new neurons.

01:26:05.200 --> 01:26:07.970
To my knowledge there's
no direct relationship

01:26:07.970 --> 01:26:10.440
between exercise and stimulating

01:26:10.440 --> 01:26:12.973
the production of new
neurons in the brain.

01:26:14.180 --> 01:26:17.300
It seems that it's the
improvement in blood flow

01:26:17.300 --> 01:26:18.960
that also relate to improvements

01:26:18.960 --> 01:26:20.500
in things like lymphatic flow,

01:26:20.500 --> 01:26:22.890
the circulation of lymph
fluid within the brain

01:26:22.890 --> 01:26:24.940
that are enhancing neurogenesis

01:26:24.940 --> 01:26:27.870
and that neurogenesis,
it appears is important.

01:26:27.870 --> 01:26:31.110
Now in fairness to the
landscape of neuroscience

01:26:31.110 --> 01:26:34.080
and my colleagues at
Stanford and elsewhere.

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:36.370
There is a lot of debate
as to whether or not

01:26:36.370 --> 01:26:41.370
there is much if any neurogenesis
in the adult human brain.

01:26:42.140 --> 01:26:44.810
But regardless, I think
the data are quite clear

01:26:44.810 --> 01:26:47.934
that the 180 to 200 minutes minimum

01:26:47.934 --> 01:26:51.000
of cardiovascular exercise
is going to be important

01:26:51.000 --> 01:26:52.600
for other health metrics.

01:26:52.600 --> 01:26:55.560
Now it is clear that
exercise can impact learning

01:26:55.560 --> 01:26:58.200
and memory through other non-neurogenesis,

01:26:58.200 --> 01:27:00.635
non-neuron type mechanisms.

01:27:00.635 --> 01:27:02.060
And one of the more exciting ones

01:27:02.060 --> 01:27:04.450
that has been studied over the years

01:27:04.450 --> 01:27:09.450
is this notion of hormones
from bone traveling

01:27:09.510 --> 01:27:11.050
in the blood stream to the brain

01:27:11.050 --> 01:27:13.350
and enhancing the function
of the hippocampus.

01:27:14.392 --> 01:27:17.550
If the words hormones from
bones is surprising to you,

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:19.590
I'm here to tell you that yes, indeed,

01:27:19.590 --> 01:27:22.190
your bones make hormones.

01:27:22.190 --> 01:27:24.030
We call these endocrine effects.

01:27:24.030 --> 01:27:28.340
So in biology we hear
about autocrine, paracrine,

01:27:28.340 --> 01:27:29.400
and endocrine.

01:27:29.400 --> 01:27:32.780
Those different terms
refer to over what distance

01:27:32.780 --> 01:27:35.660
a given chemical has an affect on a cell.

01:27:35.660 --> 01:27:38.170
For instance, a cell can
have an affect on itself.

01:27:38.170 --> 01:27:40.640
It can have an affect on
immediately neighboring cells

01:27:40.640 --> 01:27:43.440
or it can have an affect on both itself,

01:27:43.440 --> 01:27:45.480
immediately neighboring
cells and cells far,

01:27:45.480 --> 01:27:47.325
far away in the body.

01:27:47.325 --> 01:27:50.280
And that last example of a given chemical

01:27:50.280 --> 01:27:54.280
or substance having and affect
on the cell that produced it

01:27:54.280 --> 01:27:56.420
plus neighboring cells,
plus cells far away

01:27:56.420 --> 01:27:57.570
is an endocrine effect.

01:27:57.570 --> 01:28:01.380
And a lot of hormones, not
all, work in this fashion.

01:28:01.380 --> 01:28:04.650
Hence why we sometimes hear
about endocrine and hormone

01:28:04.650 --> 01:28:06.053
as kind of synonymous terms.

01:28:07.270 --> 01:28:10.940
Your bones make chemicals that
travel in the blood stream

01:28:10.940 --> 01:28:12.260
and have these endocrine effects.

01:28:12.260 --> 01:28:14.030
So they're effectively acting as hormones.

01:28:14.030 --> 01:28:17.310
And one such chemical is
something called osteocalcin.

01:28:17.310 --> 01:28:19.610
Now these findings arrived
to us through various labs

01:28:19.610 --> 01:28:20.910
but one of the more important labs

01:28:20.910 --> 01:28:22.710
for the sake of this discussion today

01:28:22.710 --> 01:28:26.510
is the laboratory of Eric Kandel
at Columbia Medical School.

01:28:26.510 --> 01:28:29.180
Eric is now, I believe
in his mid to late 90s,

01:28:29.180 --> 01:28:30.340
still very sharp.

01:28:30.340 --> 01:28:32.220
And has studied learning and memory.

01:28:32.220 --> 01:28:34.890
It also turns out that
he is an avid swimmer.

01:28:34.890 --> 01:28:37.580
Now, I happen to know
that Eric swims anywhere

01:28:37.580 --> 01:28:40.000
from a half a mile to a mile a day.

01:28:40.000 --> 01:28:42.910
And again, this is anecdotal.

01:28:42.910 --> 01:28:44.750
I'm not referring to the
published data just yet.

01:28:44.750 --> 01:28:48.310
But he credits that
exercise as one of the ways

01:28:48.310 --> 01:28:50.160
in which he keeps his brain sharp

01:28:50.160 --> 01:28:52.410
and has indeed kept his brain sharp

01:28:52.410 --> 01:28:54.220
for many, many decades.

01:28:54.220 --> 01:28:56.010
And as I mentioned before,
he's well into his 90s.

01:28:56.010 --> 01:28:57.410
So pretty impressive.

01:28:57.410 --> 01:28:59.380
His laboratory has studied the effects

01:28:59.380 --> 01:29:02.800
of exercise on hippocampal
function and memory.

01:29:02.800 --> 01:29:05.270
And other laboratories
have done that as well.

01:29:05.270 --> 01:29:08.490
And what they've found is
that cardiovascular exercise

01:29:08.490 --> 01:29:10.230
and perhaps other forms of exercise too,

01:29:10.230 --> 01:29:13.200
but mainly cardiovascular exercise creates

01:29:13.200 --> 01:29:16.060
the release of osteocalcin from the bones

01:29:16.060 --> 01:29:18.640
that travels to the
brain and to sub regions

01:29:18.640 --> 01:29:21.270
of the hippocampus and encourages

01:29:21.270 --> 01:29:25.220
the electrical activity and
formation and maintenance

01:29:25.220 --> 01:29:27.460
of connections within the hippocampus

01:29:27.460 --> 01:29:29.350
and keeps the hippocampus functioning well

01:29:29.350 --> 01:29:31.480
in order to lay down new memories.

01:29:31.480 --> 01:29:33.520
Now osteocalcin has a lot of effects

01:29:33.520 --> 01:29:36.370
besides just improving the
function of the hippocampus.

01:29:36.370 --> 01:29:39.400
Osteocalcin is involved
in bone growth itself.

01:29:39.400 --> 01:29:41.310
It's involved in hormone regulation.

01:29:41.310 --> 01:29:42.950
In fact, there's really nice evidence

01:29:42.950 --> 01:29:46.020
that it can regulate testosterone
and estrogen production

01:29:46.020 --> 01:29:47.530
by the testes and ovaries.

01:29:47.530 --> 01:29:49.910
And a bunch of other effects
in other organs of the body.

01:29:49.910 --> 01:29:52.140
Because again, it's acting
in this endocrine manner.

01:29:52.140 --> 01:29:54.900
It's arriving from bone to a lot

01:29:54.900 --> 01:29:56.700
of different organs to have effects.

01:29:57.570 --> 01:29:59.890
Load bearing exercise, in particular,

01:29:59.890 --> 01:30:02.330
turns out to be important for inducing

01:30:02.330 --> 01:30:03.760
the release of osteocalcin.

01:30:03.760 --> 01:30:06.850
And when you think about
this, it makes sense.

01:30:06.850 --> 01:30:08.970
A nervous system exists
for a lot of reasons,

01:30:08.970 --> 01:30:10.270
to sense, perceive, etc.

01:30:10.270 --> 01:30:11.200
You've got taste, you've got smell,

01:30:11.200 --> 01:30:12.190
you've got hearing.

01:30:12.190 --> 01:30:15.120
But the vast majority
of brain real estate,

01:30:15.120 --> 01:30:18.100
especially in humans, is
dedicated to two things.

01:30:18.100 --> 01:30:19.890
One, vision.

01:30:19.890 --> 01:30:21.050
We have an enormous amount

01:30:21.050 --> 01:30:23.380
of brain real estate devoted to vision.

01:30:23.380 --> 01:30:25.610
Certainly compared to other senses.

01:30:25.610 --> 01:30:27.070
And to movement.

01:30:27.070 --> 01:30:30.140
The ability to generate
course movements of the body.

01:30:30.140 --> 01:30:32.520
The ability, excuse me,
to generate fine movements

01:30:32.520 --> 01:30:33.620
of the body, like the digits,

01:30:33.620 --> 01:30:35.640
or to wink one eye, or to tilt your head

01:30:35.640 --> 01:30:37.380
in a particular way, or move your lips

01:30:37.380 --> 01:30:39.110
and move your face and do
all sorts of different things

01:30:39.110 --> 01:30:42.120
in a very nuanced and detailed way.

01:30:42.120 --> 01:30:43.470
So much of our brain real estate

01:30:43.470 --> 01:30:47.070
is devoted to movement
that it's been hypothesized

01:30:47.070 --> 01:30:49.470
for more than a half century,

01:30:49.470 --> 01:30:51.150
but especially in recent years

01:30:51.150 --> 01:30:52.340
as we've learned more about the function

01:30:52.340 --> 01:30:54.740
of the brain in a really
detailed circuit level,

01:30:55.729 --> 01:30:58.070
that the relationship
between the brain and body

01:30:58.070 --> 01:31:00.860
and the maintenance and perhaps even

01:31:00.860 --> 01:31:02.930
the improvement of the
neural circuitry in the brain

01:31:02.930 --> 01:31:05.660
depends on our body
movements and the signal

01:31:05.660 --> 01:31:08.490
from the body that our
brain is still moving.

01:31:08.490 --> 01:31:09.323
So think about that.

01:31:09.323 --> 01:31:10.820
How would your brain know if your body

01:31:10.820 --> 01:31:12.650
was moving regularly and how would it know

01:31:12.650 --> 01:31:13.630
how much it was moving?

01:31:13.630 --> 01:31:15.970
How would it know which
limbs it was moving?

01:31:15.970 --> 01:31:18.150
Well, you could say, if the heart rate

01:31:18.150 --> 01:31:20.230
is increased then the blood
flow will be increased

01:31:20.230 --> 01:31:21.970
and then the brain will know.

01:31:21.970 --> 01:31:24.630
Ah, but how does your brain know

01:31:24.630 --> 01:31:27.310
that its increased blood
flow due to movement

01:31:27.310 --> 01:31:30.030
and not to, for instance,
just stress, right?

01:31:30.030 --> 01:31:31.130
Maybe you actually can't move

01:31:31.130 --> 01:31:32.330
and you're very stressed about that

01:31:32.330 --> 01:31:33.820
and so the increased blood flow

01:31:33.820 --> 01:31:37.303
is simply a consequence
of increased stress.

01:31:38.820 --> 01:31:42.930
The fact that osteocalcin
is released from bone

01:31:42.930 --> 01:31:44.750
and in particular can be released

01:31:44.750 --> 01:31:46.930
in response to load bearing exercise.

01:31:46.930 --> 01:31:49.240
So this would be running,
again weightlifting

01:31:49.240 --> 01:31:50.510
hasn't been tested directly,

01:31:50.510 --> 01:31:52.640
but one would imagine
anything that involves

01:31:52.640 --> 01:31:55.127
jumping and landing, or weightlifting,

01:31:55.127 --> 01:31:58.433
or body weight movements
and things of that sort.

01:31:59.340 --> 01:32:01.870
That's a signal to release osteocalcin,

01:32:01.870 --> 01:32:04.000
and we know that signal occurs.

01:32:04.000 --> 01:32:07.320
That is directly reflective

01:32:07.320 --> 01:32:09.920
of the fact that the body was moving

01:32:09.920 --> 01:32:11.230
and moving in particular ways.

01:32:11.230 --> 01:32:13.870
In fact, you could imagine that big bones

01:32:13.870 --> 01:32:16.280
like your femur are going
to release more osteocalcin

01:32:16.280 --> 01:32:18.330
or be in a position to
release more osteocalcin

01:32:18.330 --> 01:32:21.320
then fine movements like
the movements of the digits.

01:32:21.320 --> 01:32:24.010
And this idea that the body is constantly

01:32:24.010 --> 01:32:26.220
signaling to the brain about the status

01:32:26.220 --> 01:32:29.130
of the body and the
varying needs of the brain

01:32:29.130 --> 01:32:31.410
to update its brain circuitry,

01:32:31.410 --> 01:32:34.890
is a really attractive
idea that fits entirely

01:32:34.890 --> 01:32:37.910
with the biology of exercise, osteocalcin,

01:32:37.910 --> 01:32:39.570
and hippocampal function.

01:32:39.570 --> 01:32:42.090
I do want to mention that I'm not

01:32:42.090 --> 01:32:43.790
the first to raise this hypothesis.

01:32:43.790 --> 01:32:45.700
This hypothesis actually was discussed

01:32:45.700 --> 01:32:48.100
in a fair amount of detail by John Ratey

01:32:48.100 --> 01:32:50.330
who's a professor at
Harvard Medical School.

01:32:50.330 --> 01:32:51.750
He wrote a book called, "Spark"

01:32:51.750 --> 01:32:54.544
which was one of the early books

01:32:54.544 --> 01:32:56.007
at least from an academic
about brain plasticity

01:32:56.007 --> 01:32:57.680
and the relationship between exercise

01:32:57.680 --> 01:32:58.970
and movement and plasticity.

01:32:58.970 --> 01:33:01.990
And John, who I have the
good fortune to know,

01:33:01.990 --> 01:33:03.900
has described to me experiments,

01:33:03.900 --> 01:33:06.810
or I should say observations of species

01:33:06.810 --> 01:33:09.440
of ocean dwelling animals that have,

01:33:09.440 --> 01:33:11.100
at least for the early part of their life,

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:14.210
a very robust and
complicated nervous system.

01:33:14.210 --> 01:33:16.690
But then these particular animals

01:33:16.690 --> 01:33:19.430
are in the habit of
plopping down unto a rock.

01:33:19.430 --> 01:33:21.770
They find kind of a safe, comfy space

01:33:21.770 --> 01:33:24.040
and they actually stick to that rock

01:33:24.040 --> 01:33:27.000
and they don't move anymore
for a certain portion,

01:33:27.000 --> 01:33:29.140
I should say the later
portion of their life.

01:33:29.140 --> 01:33:31.650
And it is at the transition
between moving a lot

01:33:31.650 --> 01:33:33.580
and being stationary that those animals

01:33:33.580 --> 01:33:36.437
actually digest their own brain.

01:33:36.437 --> 01:33:38.960
The literally metabolize a good portion

01:33:38.960 --> 01:33:41.050
of their nervous system
because they decide, oh,

01:33:41.050 --> 01:33:42.240
don't need this anymore.

01:33:42.240 --> 01:33:46.320
And gobble it up, use it
for its nutritional value

01:33:46.320 --> 01:33:49.810
and then sit there like a
moron version of themselves

01:33:50.660 --> 01:33:53.576
with a limited amount of brain tissue

01:33:53.576 --> 01:33:55.460
because they don't need to move anymore.

01:33:55.460 --> 01:33:57.160
Now, I certainly don't
want to give the message

01:33:57.160 --> 01:33:59.250
that just moving, just exercise,

01:33:59.250 --> 01:34:01.510
is sufficient to keep
the neural architecture

01:34:01.510 --> 01:34:04.690
of your brain healthy,
young, and able to learn.

01:34:04.690 --> 01:34:06.430
While that might be true,

01:34:06.430 --> 01:34:08.210
it's also important to actually engage

01:34:08.210 --> 01:34:10.260
in attempts to learn new material.

01:34:10.260 --> 01:34:13.550
Either physical material,
so new types of movements

01:34:13.550 --> 01:34:17.020
and skills and or new types
of cognitive information.

01:34:17.020 --> 01:34:20.720
Languages, mathematics,
history, current events.

01:34:20.720 --> 01:34:23.760
All sorts of things
that involve your brain.

01:34:23.760 --> 01:34:26.820
Nonetheless, it's clear
that physical movement

01:34:26.820 --> 01:34:29.470
and cognitive ability and the potential

01:34:29.470 --> 01:34:31.390
to enhance cognitive ability

01:34:31.390 --> 01:34:33.310
and the ability to learn
new physical skills

01:34:33.310 --> 01:34:34.990
are intimately connected.

01:34:34.990 --> 01:34:38.430
And osteocalcin appears
to be at least one way

01:34:38.430 --> 01:34:40.090
in which that brain-body relationship

01:34:40.090 --> 01:34:41.720
is established and maintained.

01:34:41.720 --> 01:34:45.170
So given the information about
osteocalcin and movement,

01:34:45.170 --> 01:34:49.010
and given the information
about spiking adrenaline late

01:34:49.010 --> 01:34:52.330
or after a period of an attempt to learn,

01:34:52.330 --> 01:34:55.150
you might be asking when is
the best time to exercise?

01:34:55.150 --> 01:34:58.060
Now unfortunately, that
has not been addressed

01:34:58.060 --> 01:34:59.840
in a lot of varying detail,

01:34:59.840 --> 01:35:02.780
where every sort of variation on the theme

01:35:02.780 --> 01:35:03.710
has been carried out.

01:35:03.710 --> 01:35:05.760
And yet, Wendy Suzuki's lab has done

01:35:05.760 --> 01:35:08.070
really beautiful experiments where

01:35:08.070 --> 01:35:11.890
they have people exercise,
generally it was in the morning.

01:35:11.890 --> 01:35:14.660
But at other periods of the day as well.

01:35:14.660 --> 01:35:17.900
And what they find is
that at least as late as

01:35:17.900 --> 01:35:20.730
two hours after that exercise,

01:35:20.730 --> 01:35:23.270
there is an enhancement
in learning and memory.

01:35:23.270 --> 01:35:25.240
Now I want to be clear, we don't know

01:35:25.240 --> 01:35:27.140
whether or not that exercise led

01:35:27.140 --> 01:35:29.620
to big increases in adrenaline.

01:35:29.620 --> 01:35:32.210
It may be that those forms of exercise

01:35:32.210 --> 01:35:35.540
were modest enough, or didn't
challenge people enough

01:35:35.540 --> 01:35:37.620
that they merely got a
lot of blood flow going

01:35:37.620 --> 01:35:38.547
and that the improvements in learning

01:35:38.547 --> 01:35:40.420
and memory were related to blood flow

01:35:40.420 --> 01:35:43.960
and we presume increases in osteocalcin.

01:35:43.960 --> 01:35:45.650
However, you could imagine a couple

01:35:45.650 --> 01:35:48.380
of different logical protocols based

01:35:48.380 --> 01:35:50.100
on what we've talked about.

01:35:50.100 --> 01:35:51.920
Let's say you were going
to do a form of exercise

01:35:51.920 --> 01:35:53.590
that was going to spike adrenaline a lot.

01:35:53.590 --> 01:35:56.450
So this would be exercise
that really challenges

01:35:56.450 --> 01:35:57.550
your system and forces you to kind

01:35:57.550 --> 01:35:59.000
of push through a burn.

01:35:59.000 --> 01:36:00.860
Right, so here I'm mainly thinking about

01:36:00.860 --> 01:36:02.010
cardiovascular exercise.

01:36:02.010 --> 01:36:04.800
But it could even be yoga,

01:36:04.800 --> 01:36:07.240
it could be resistance training.

01:36:07.240 --> 01:36:09.010
If it's going to give you a big spike

01:36:09.010 --> 01:36:12.410
in adrenaline, it's going
to take some serious effort,

01:36:12.410 --> 01:36:16.140
then logically speaking you
would want to place that

01:36:16.140 --> 01:36:18.480
after a learning about
in order to increase

01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:19.313
learning and memory.

01:36:19.313 --> 01:36:21.780
However, if you're using the exercise

01:36:21.780 --> 01:36:23.520
in order to enhance blood flow

01:36:23.520 --> 01:36:26.140
and to enhance osteocalcin release.

01:36:26.140 --> 01:36:28.810
In efforts to augment the
function of your hippocampus,

01:36:28.810 --> 01:36:30.520
I think it stands to reason that doing

01:36:30.520 --> 01:36:33.250
that exercise sometime within

01:36:33.250 --> 01:36:36.920
the hour to three hours
preceding an attempt to learn

01:36:36.920 --> 01:36:37.920
makes a lot of sense.

01:36:37.920 --> 01:36:39.530
And there I'm basing it on the human data

01:36:39.530 --> 01:36:41.110
from Wendy Suzuki's lab.

01:36:41.110 --> 01:36:43.400
I'm basing it on the
studies from Eric Kandel

01:36:43.400 --> 01:36:45.370
and from others labs.

01:36:45.370 --> 01:36:47.220
Again, right now, there hasn't been

01:36:47.220 --> 01:36:49.360
an evaluation of a lot
of different protocols

01:36:49.360 --> 01:36:53.540
to arrive at the peer-reviewed
laboratory super protocol.

01:36:53.540 --> 01:36:55.680
However, since what we're talking about

01:36:55.680 --> 01:36:58.060
is using activities
like exercise that most

01:36:58.060 --> 01:37:00.550
of us probably, perhaps all of us,

01:37:00.550 --> 01:37:02.540
should be doing regularly anyway.

01:37:02.540 --> 01:37:05.330
And I do believe most
if not all of us should

01:37:05.330 --> 01:37:06.480
regularly be trying to learn

01:37:06.480 --> 01:37:08.240
and keep our brain functioning well

01:37:08.240 --> 01:37:09.073
and acquire new knowledge.

01:37:09.073 --> 01:37:10.780
Because it's just a
wonderful part of life.

01:37:10.780 --> 01:37:12.600
And there is evidence
that actually can keep

01:37:12.600 --> 01:37:14.650
your brain young, so to speak.

01:37:14.650 --> 01:37:18.340
Well then, exercising either before

01:37:18.340 --> 01:37:21.590
or after a learning about
makes a lot of sense.

01:37:21.590 --> 01:37:24.220
With the emphasis on
after a learning about

01:37:24.220 --> 01:37:26.510
if the form of exercise
spikes a lot of adrenaline

01:37:26.510 --> 01:37:28.470
for all the reasons we
talked about before.

01:37:28.470 --> 01:37:30.110
Okay, so we've talked about two major

01:37:30.110 --> 01:37:32.240
categories of protocols to improve memory

01:37:32.240 --> 01:37:35.270
that are grounded in quality,
peer reviewed science.

01:37:35.270 --> 01:37:37.780
And there is yet another third protocol

01:37:37.780 --> 01:37:39.490
that we'll talk about in a few minutes.

01:37:39.490 --> 01:37:41.141
But before we do that,

01:37:41.141 --> 01:37:43.380
I want to briefly touch
on an aspect of memory,

01:37:43.380 --> 01:37:44.810
in fact, two aspects of memory

01:37:44.810 --> 01:37:47.010
that I get a lot of questions about.

01:37:47.010 --> 01:37:50.830
The first one is photographic memory.

01:37:50.830 --> 01:37:52.960
To be clear, there are people out there

01:37:52.960 --> 01:37:54.800
who have a true photographic memory.

01:37:54.800 --> 01:37:56.630
They can look at a page of text,

01:37:56.630 --> 01:37:57.880
they can scan it with their eyes,

01:37:57.880 --> 01:38:00.800
and they can essentially
commit that to memory

01:38:00.800 --> 01:38:03.230
with very little if any effort.

01:38:03.230 --> 01:38:04.970
While it might seem that
having a photographic memory

01:38:04.970 --> 01:38:06.460
is a very attractive skill to have,

01:38:06.460 --> 01:38:09.070
I should caution you
against believing that

01:38:09.070 --> 01:38:10.740
because it turns out that people

01:38:10.740 --> 01:38:13.300
with true photographic memory
are often very challenged

01:38:13.300 --> 01:38:15.600
at remembering things that they hear.

01:38:15.600 --> 01:38:17.430
And often times are not so good

01:38:17.430 --> 01:38:18.820
at learning physical skills.

01:38:18.820 --> 01:38:21.030
It's not always the case,
but often that's the case.

01:38:21.030 --> 01:38:22.750
So be careful what you wish for.

01:38:22.750 --> 01:38:24.450
If you do have a photographic memory

01:38:24.450 --> 01:38:26.620
there are certain professions
that lend themselves

01:38:26.620 --> 01:38:29.680
particularly well to you.

01:38:29.680 --> 01:38:31.810
And indeed a lot of people
with photographic memory

01:38:31.810 --> 01:38:34.970
have to find a profession
and have to move through life

01:38:34.970 --> 01:38:37.730
in a way that is in concert

01:38:37.730 --> 01:38:38.890
with that photographic memory.

01:38:38.890 --> 01:38:41.270
So again, it's a super ability,

01:38:41.270 --> 01:38:43.960
it's a hyper ability and
yet it's not necessarily one

01:38:43.960 --> 01:38:46.380
that is desirable for most people.

01:38:46.380 --> 01:38:47.830
There's also this category
of what are called

01:38:47.830 --> 01:38:49.490
super recognizers.

01:38:49.490 --> 01:38:51.050
These people are, I should mention,

01:38:51.050 --> 01:38:53.370
highly employable by government agencies.

01:38:53.370 --> 01:38:57.950
These are people that have
an absolutely astonishing

01:38:57.950 --> 01:39:02.010
ability to recognize faces and
to match faces to templates.

01:39:02.010 --> 01:39:04.250
They can look at a photograph

01:39:04.250 --> 01:39:07.380
of say somebody on a most wanted list

01:39:07.380 --> 01:39:10.730
and then they can look at video footage

01:39:10.730 --> 01:39:12.640
of let's say an airport, or a mall,

01:39:12.640 --> 01:39:15.290
or a city street at fairly low resolution

01:39:15.290 --> 01:39:17.920
and they can spot the person who's face

01:39:17.920 --> 01:39:20.400
matches that photograph
that they looked at.

01:39:20.400 --> 01:39:24.160
Even if that video or other footage

01:39:24.160 --> 01:39:27.230
is of people's profiles or even

01:39:27.230 --> 01:39:28.490
the tops of their heads and just

01:39:28.490 --> 01:39:29.430
a portion of their forehead.

01:39:29.430 --> 01:39:32.200
These people have just
an incredible ability

01:39:32.200 --> 01:39:34.470
to recognize faces and to template match.

01:39:34.470 --> 01:39:36.610
And again, these people often will take

01:39:36.610 --> 01:39:39.790
jobs with agencies where this
sort of thing is important.

01:39:39.790 --> 01:39:41.490
Some of you out there probably

01:39:41.490 --> 01:39:44.180
are super recognizers and
may or may not notice it.

01:39:44.180 --> 01:39:45.320
If you've ever had the experience

01:39:45.320 --> 01:39:47.650
of watching a movie and
thought to yourself,

01:39:47.650 --> 01:39:52.420
wow, her mouth looks so
much like my cousin's mouth.

01:39:52.420 --> 01:39:54.760
Or you look at a character in a movie

01:39:54.760 --> 01:39:57.350
or a television show and you think,

01:39:57.350 --> 01:40:01.720
wow, they look almost like the
younger sister of so and so.

01:40:01.720 --> 01:40:05.950
Well, then it's very
likely that you have this,

01:40:05.950 --> 01:40:09.110
or at least a mild form of
this super recognizer ability.

01:40:09.110 --> 01:40:11.690
That is not memory per se.

01:40:11.690 --> 01:40:14.020
That is the hyper functioning of an area

01:40:14.020 --> 01:40:17.000
of the brain that we
call the fusiform gyrus.

01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:18.600
The fusiform gyrus is literally

01:40:18.600 --> 01:40:22.920
a face recognition area, and
a face template matching area.

01:40:22.920 --> 01:40:25.880
And it harbors neurons that
respond to faces generally.

01:40:25.880 --> 01:40:28.530
So as humans and other non-human primates,

01:40:28.530 --> 01:40:31.730
we care a lot about faces
and their emotional content.

01:40:31.730 --> 01:40:35.060
And the identity of faces
is super important to us

01:40:35.060 --> 01:40:38.240
for all the kinds of reasons
that are probably obvious.

01:40:38.240 --> 01:40:40.050
Knowing who's friend, who's foe.

01:40:40.050 --> 01:40:40.940
Who do you know well?

01:40:40.940 --> 01:40:43.090
Who's famous, who's not famous?

01:40:43.090 --> 01:40:43.923
Etc.

01:40:43.923 --> 01:40:45.740
That is not memory, per se.

01:40:45.740 --> 01:40:48.140
And yet, if you're a super recognizer,

01:40:48.140 --> 01:40:51.940
or I guess you could call it
a moderate face recognizer

01:40:51.940 --> 01:40:54.120
or not very good at recognizing faces

01:40:54.120 --> 01:40:55.230
because indeed, there are some people

01:40:55.230 --> 01:40:56.940
that are kind of face blind.

01:40:56.940 --> 01:40:59.090
They don't actually recognize people

01:40:59.090 --> 01:40:59.923
when they walk in the room.

01:40:59.923 --> 01:41:01.080
I used to work with somebody like this.

01:41:01.080 --> 01:41:02.790
I'd walk into his office and he'd say,

01:41:02.790 --> 01:41:05.010
are you Rich or are you Andrew?

01:41:05.010 --> 01:41:07.100
And I would say, well am I rich, rich.

01:41:07.100 --> 01:41:08.250
Like, you know, wealth rich?

01:41:08.250 --> 01:41:09.083
No.

01:41:09.083 --> 01:41:11.610
And he'd say, no, are you
Richard or are you Andrew?

01:41:11.610 --> 01:41:12.770
And I'd say, I'm Andrew.

01:41:12.770 --> 01:41:14.135
We know each other really well.

01:41:14.135 --> 01:41:15.070
And he'd say, oh I'm sorry.

01:41:15.070 --> 01:41:16.130
I'm kind of face blind.

01:41:16.130 --> 01:41:18.448
And it actually tended
to be better or worse

01:41:18.448 --> 01:41:21.240
depending on how much he was working.

01:41:21.240 --> 01:41:24.110
Ironically, the more rested he was

01:41:24.110 --> 01:41:25.570
the more face blind he would become.

01:41:25.570 --> 01:41:27.210
So it wasn't a sleep deprivation thing.

01:41:27.210 --> 01:41:28.310
That exists, that's out there.

01:41:28.310 --> 01:41:30.220
There's the full constellation

01:41:30.220 --> 01:41:32.290
of people's ability to recognize faces.

01:41:32.290 --> 01:41:33.420
That's not really memory.

01:41:33.420 --> 01:41:37.710
And yet, visual function is
a profoundly powerful way

01:41:37.710 --> 01:41:39.190
in which we can enhance our memories.

01:41:39.190 --> 01:41:41.830
So whether or not you're a
super recognizer of faces,

01:41:41.830 --> 01:41:43.580
whether or not you are face blind

01:41:43.580 --> 01:41:45.200
or anything in between.

01:41:45.200 --> 01:41:47.440
Next I'm going to tell you about a study

01:41:47.440 --> 01:41:52.190
which points out the immense
value of visual images

01:41:52.190 --> 01:41:54.170
for laying down memories.

01:41:54.170 --> 01:41:56.150
And you can leverage this information

01:41:56.150 --> 01:41:58.340
and this involves both
the taking of photographs,

01:41:58.340 --> 01:42:00.140
something that's quite easily

01:42:00.140 --> 01:42:01.610
done these days with your phone.

01:42:01.610 --> 01:42:04.270
As well as your ability
to take mental photographs

01:42:04.270 --> 01:42:06.740
by literally snapping your eyelids shut.

01:42:06.740 --> 01:42:08.470
So I just briefly want
to describe this paper

01:42:08.470 --> 01:42:10.820
because it provides a
tool that you can leverage

01:42:10.820 --> 01:42:14.150
in your attempt to learn
and remember things better.

01:42:14.150 --> 01:42:17.010
The title of this paper
is Photographic Memory,

01:42:17.010 --> 01:42:19.170
the Effects of our Volitional Photo-Taking

01:42:19.170 --> 01:42:22.833
on Memory for Visual and Auditory
Aspects of an Experience.

01:42:23.730 --> 01:42:26.630
I really like this paper because it refers

01:42:26.630 --> 01:42:28.760
to photographic memory not in the context

01:42:28.760 --> 01:42:31.150
of photographic memory
that we normally hear about

01:42:31.150 --> 01:42:32.790
where people are truly photographic,

01:42:32.790 --> 01:42:35.630
look at a page and somehow
absorb all that information

01:42:35.630 --> 01:42:36.720
and commit it to memory.

01:42:36.720 --> 01:42:39.650
But rather the use of camera photographs

01:42:39.650 --> 01:42:42.880
or the use of mental camera photographs.

01:42:42.880 --> 01:42:45.620
Literally looking at
something deciding, blink,

01:42:45.620 --> 01:42:49.420
snapping a, so to speak,
snapping a snapshot

01:42:49.420 --> 01:42:50.930
of whatever it is that you are looking at

01:42:50.930 --> 01:42:52.470
and remembering the content.

01:42:52.470 --> 01:42:53.670
The reason I like this paper

01:42:53.670 --> 01:42:55.550
and the reason I'm attracted to this issue

01:42:55.550 --> 01:42:57.290
of mental snapshots is this is something

01:42:57.290 --> 01:42:58.410
that I've been doing since I was a kid.

01:42:58.410 --> 01:42:59.670
I don't know why I started doing it,

01:42:59.670 --> 01:43:03.470
but every once in a while, I
would say maybe twice a year

01:43:03.470 --> 01:43:05.470
I would look at something
and decide to just

01:43:05.470 --> 01:43:07.460
snap a mental snapshot of it.

01:43:07.460 --> 01:43:09.150
And I've maintained very clear memories

01:43:09.150 --> 01:43:10.600
of those visual scenes.

01:43:10.600 --> 01:43:13.190
Two years ago I was in an Uber

01:43:13.190 --> 01:43:14.850
and I looked out the window

01:43:14.850 --> 01:43:15.683
and it was a street scene.

01:43:15.683 --> 01:43:17.370
I was actually in New York at the time

01:43:17.370 --> 01:43:21.160
and I decided for reasons
that are still unclear to me,

01:43:21.160 --> 01:43:23.440
to take a mental snapshot
of this city street image.

01:43:23.440 --> 01:43:26.360
Even though nothing interesting
in particular was happening.

01:43:26.360 --> 01:43:29.140
And I do recall that
there was a guy wearing

01:43:29.140 --> 01:43:31.480
a yellow shirt walking, there
was some construction, etc.

01:43:31.480 --> 01:43:33.740
I can still see that
image in my mind's eye

01:43:33.740 --> 01:43:35.600
because I took this mental snapshot.

01:43:35.600 --> 01:43:36.850
This paper addresses whether or not

01:43:36.850 --> 01:43:38.820
this mental snapshotting thing is real

01:43:38.820 --> 01:43:41.030
and this is something I think a lot

01:43:41.030 --> 01:43:43.020
of people will resonate with,

01:43:43.020 --> 01:43:44.810
whether or not the constant taking

01:43:44.810 --> 01:43:46.944
of pictures on our phones
or with other devices

01:43:46.944 --> 01:43:50.170
is either improving or
degrading our memory.

01:43:50.170 --> 01:43:52.600
You could imagine an argument for both.

01:43:52.600 --> 01:43:53.850
A lot of people are taking pictures

01:43:53.850 --> 01:43:55.720
that they never look at again.

01:43:55.720 --> 01:43:57.880
And so in a sense, they're outsourcing

01:43:57.880 --> 01:44:01.830
their visual memory of
events into their phone

01:44:01.830 --> 01:44:04.380
or some other device and they're not ever

01:44:04.380 --> 01:44:06.200
accessing the actual image again.

01:44:06.200 --> 01:44:07.320
They're not looking at it, right?

01:44:07.320 --> 01:44:08.700
You're not printing out those photos.

01:44:08.700 --> 01:44:10.110
You're not scanning
through your phone again.

01:44:10.110 --> 01:44:11.010
Sometimes you might do that,

01:44:11.010 --> 01:44:12.100
but most of the time people don't.

01:44:12.100 --> 01:44:13.560
Most of the photographs people are taking

01:44:13.560 --> 01:44:15.030
they're not revisiting again.

01:44:15.030 --> 01:44:16.830
So the motivation for this study was that

01:44:16.830 --> 01:44:20.600
previous experiments had shown that

01:44:20.600 --> 01:44:22.610
if people take photos of a scene

01:44:22.610 --> 01:44:26.540
or a person, or an object, that
they are actually less good

01:44:26.540 --> 01:44:29.810
at remembering the details of that scene

01:44:29.810 --> 01:44:31.263
or object, etc.

01:44:32.210 --> 01:44:34.430
This study challenged that idea

01:44:34.430 --> 01:44:37.140
and raised the hypothesis that

01:44:37.140 --> 01:44:39.210
if people are allowed to choose what

01:44:39.210 --> 01:44:42.180
they take photos of, that taking photos,

01:44:42.180 --> 01:44:44.290
again, this is with the camera,
not mental snapshotting.

01:44:44.290 --> 01:44:45.820
That taking those photos would actually

01:44:45.820 --> 01:44:48.560
enhance their memory for those objects,

01:44:48.560 --> 01:44:50.550
those places, those people, and in fact,

01:44:50.550 --> 01:44:53.150
details of those objects,
places, and people.

01:44:53.150 --> 01:44:54.950
And indeed, that's what they found.

01:44:54.950 --> 01:44:56.990
So in contrast to previous studies

01:44:56.990 --> 01:44:59.550
where people had been more or less told,

01:44:59.550 --> 01:45:01.420
take photos of these following objects,

01:45:01.420 --> 01:45:03.740
or these following people,
or these following places

01:45:03.740 --> 01:45:06.470
and then they were given a memory test

01:45:06.470 --> 01:45:07.520
at some point later.

01:45:07.520 --> 01:45:11.300
In this study people were given
volitional control, right?

01:45:11.300 --> 01:45:13.270
They were given agency in making

01:45:13.270 --> 01:45:15.050
the decision of what to take photos of.

01:45:15.050 --> 01:45:16.270
And I'll just summarize the results.

01:45:16.270 --> 01:45:17.540
We'll provide a link to this study.

01:45:17.540 --> 01:45:19.270
I should say that some of the stuff

01:45:19.270 --> 01:45:21.560
that they tested was
actually pretty challenging.

01:45:21.560 --> 01:45:23.970
Some of them were pottery and other forms

01:45:23.970 --> 01:45:26.640
of ceramics that are of
the sort that you see

01:45:26.640 --> 01:45:28.610
if you go to a big museum in a big city.

01:45:28.610 --> 01:45:29.443
And if you've ever done that,

01:45:29.443 --> 01:45:31.160
and you see all the different objects,

01:45:31.160 --> 01:45:32.640
there are a lot of
details in those objects

01:45:32.640 --> 01:45:35.350
and a lot of those
objects look a lot alike.

01:45:35.350 --> 01:45:37.710
And so someone will have two handles.

01:45:37.710 --> 01:45:38.610
Some will have one handle.

01:45:38.610 --> 01:45:39.700
The position of the handles.

01:45:39.700 --> 01:45:42.530
How broad or narrow these things are.

01:45:42.530 --> 01:45:44.510
You know, a lot of this
is pretty detailed stuff.

01:45:44.510 --> 01:45:47.320
They also took photos of other things.

01:45:47.320 --> 01:45:49.940
So basically what they found was that

01:45:49.940 --> 01:45:50.900
if people take pictures of things

01:45:50.900 --> 01:45:52.220
and they choose which things

01:45:52.220 --> 01:45:53.770
they are taking pictures of, right.

01:45:53.770 --> 01:45:55.280
It's up to them, it's volitional.

01:45:55.280 --> 01:46:00.280
That there's enhanced memory
for those objects later on.

01:46:01.870 --> 01:46:04.430
However, it degraded their ability

01:46:04.430 --> 01:46:05.793
to remember auditory information.

01:46:05.793 --> 01:46:07.440
So what this means is that when we take

01:46:07.440 --> 01:46:11.090
a picture of something, or a person,

01:46:11.090 --> 01:46:14.650
we are stamping down a
visual memory of that thing.

01:46:14.650 --> 01:46:16.880
And that makes sense, it's
a photograph after all.

01:46:16.880 --> 01:46:19.100
But we are actually inhibiting our ability

01:46:19.100 --> 01:46:22.530
to remember the auditory,
the sound component

01:46:22.530 --> 01:46:24.610
of that visual scene or
what the person was saying.

01:46:24.610 --> 01:46:25.500
Very interesting.

01:46:25.500 --> 01:46:27.660
And points to the fact
that the visual system

01:46:27.660 --> 01:46:29.180
can out compete the auditory system,

01:46:29.180 --> 01:46:31.020
at least in terms of how the hippocampus

01:46:31.020 --> 01:46:32.770
is encoding this information.

01:46:32.770 --> 01:46:34.750
The other finding I find
particularly interesting

01:46:34.750 --> 01:46:38.470
within this study is that it didn't matter

01:46:38.470 --> 01:46:40.560
whether or not they ever
looked at the photos again.

01:46:40.560 --> 01:46:42.950
So they actually had people take photos,

01:46:42.950 --> 01:46:45.640
or not take photos of different objects.

01:46:45.640 --> 01:46:47.170
They had some people keep their photos

01:46:47.170 --> 01:46:49.400
and they had other
people delete the photos.

01:46:49.400 --> 01:46:51.724
And it turns out that whether or not

01:46:51.724 --> 01:46:53.230
people kept the photos
or deleted those photos

01:46:53.230 --> 01:46:54.520
had no bearing on whether or not

01:46:54.520 --> 01:46:57.230
they were better or worse
at remembering things.

01:46:57.230 --> 01:46:59.450
They were always better
at remembering them

01:46:59.450 --> 01:47:01.050
as compared to not taking photos of them.

01:47:01.050 --> 01:47:01.890
What does this mean?

01:47:01.890 --> 01:47:03.630
It means that if you really
want to remember something

01:47:03.630 --> 01:47:07.640
or somebody, take a photo
of that thing or person.

01:47:07.640 --> 01:47:10.140
Pay attention while you take the photo.

01:47:10.140 --> 01:47:11.280
But it doesn't really matter if you look

01:47:11.280 --> 01:47:13.049
at the photo again.

01:47:13.049 --> 01:47:15.610
Somehow the process of taking that photo,

01:47:15.610 --> 01:47:17.415
probably looking at it.

01:47:17.415 --> 01:47:19.030
You know, in a camera typically we'd say

01:47:19.030 --> 01:47:21.320
through the viewfinder or now
because of digital cameras

01:47:21.320 --> 01:47:23.650
on the screen on the back of that camera,

01:47:23.650 --> 01:47:27.070
or on your phone, that
framing up of the photograph

01:47:27.070 --> 01:47:29.060
stamps down a visual image in your mind

01:47:29.060 --> 01:47:32.040
that is more robust at serving a memory

01:47:32.040 --> 01:47:33.480
then had you just looked at that thing

01:47:33.480 --> 01:47:35.020
with your own eyes.

01:47:35.020 --> 01:47:36.710
Very interesting and it raises all sorts

01:47:36.710 --> 01:47:38.830
of questions for me about whether or not

01:47:38.830 --> 01:47:41.020
it's because you're
framing up a small aperture

01:47:41.020 --> 01:47:42.870
or a small portion of the visual scene.

01:47:42.870 --> 01:47:44.470
That's one logical interpretation,

01:47:44.470 --> 01:47:45.950
although they didn't test that.

01:47:45.950 --> 01:47:47.730
I should also say that they found

01:47:47.730 --> 01:47:48.660
that whether or not that you looked

01:47:48.660 --> 01:47:50.610
at a photo that you took,

01:47:50.610 --> 01:47:51.910
or whether or not you deleted it

01:47:51.910 --> 01:47:53.750
and never looked at it again,

01:47:53.750 --> 01:47:55.790
didn't just enhance visual memory

01:47:55.790 --> 01:47:59.030
or the memory from the visual
components of that image

01:47:59.030 --> 01:48:01.730
but it always reduced your ability

01:48:01.730 --> 01:48:04.820
to remember sounds associated
with that experience.

01:48:04.820 --> 01:48:06.330
So that's interesting.

01:48:06.330 --> 01:48:08.150
And then last but not least,

01:48:08.150 --> 01:48:10.440
and perhaps most
interesting, at least to me,

01:48:10.440 --> 01:48:12.450
was the fact that you didn't even need

01:48:12.450 --> 01:48:14.610
a camera to see this effect.

01:48:14.610 --> 01:48:16.530
If subjects looked at something

01:48:16.530 --> 01:48:19.370
and took a mental
photograph of that thing,

01:48:19.370 --> 01:48:22.684
it enhanced their visual
memory of that thing

01:48:22.684 --> 01:48:25.300
significantly more than had they not taken

01:48:25.300 --> 01:48:26.640
a mental picture.

01:48:26.640 --> 01:48:30.320
In fact, it increased
their memory of that thing

01:48:30.320 --> 01:48:32.810
almost as much as taking
an actual photograph

01:48:32.810 --> 01:48:34.400
with an actual camera.

01:48:34.400 --> 01:48:36.380
And the reason I find this so interesting

01:48:36.380 --> 01:48:40.530
is that a lot of what we
try and learn is visual.

01:48:40.530 --> 01:48:41.940
And for a lot of people,

01:48:41.940 --> 01:48:44.670
the ability to learn visual information

01:48:44.670 --> 01:48:45.950
feels challenging.

01:48:45.950 --> 01:48:47.490
And we'll look at something and we'll try

01:48:47.490 --> 01:48:49.920
and create some detailed
understanding of it.

01:48:49.920 --> 01:48:51.290
We'll try and understand the relationships

01:48:51.290 --> 01:48:53.410
between things in that scene.

01:48:53.410 --> 01:48:54.980
It does appear based on this study

01:48:54.980 --> 01:48:58.440
that the mere decision to
take a mental snapshot,

01:48:58.440 --> 01:48:59.920
like, okay I'm going to blink my eyelids

01:48:59.920 --> 01:49:01.740
and I'm going to take a snapshot
of whatever it is I see,

01:49:01.740 --> 01:49:04.280
can actually stamp down a visual memory

01:49:04.280 --> 01:49:07.050
much in the same way that
a camera can stamp down

01:49:07.050 --> 01:49:07.883
a visual memory.

01:49:07.883 --> 01:49:10.710
Of course, through vastly
distinct mechanisms.

01:49:10.710 --> 01:49:13.240
No discussion of memory would be complete

01:49:13.240 --> 01:49:17.160
without a discussion of the
ever intriguing phenomenon

01:49:17.160 --> 01:49:19.300
known as deja vu.

01:49:19.300 --> 01:49:21.790
This is a sense that we've
experienced something before

01:49:21.790 --> 01:49:23.530
but we can't quite put our finger on it.

01:49:23.530 --> 01:49:25.030
Where and when did it happen?

01:49:25.030 --> 01:49:27.290
Or the sense that we've
been someplace before.

01:49:27.290 --> 01:49:31.047
Or that we are in a
familiar state or place

01:49:31.047 --> 01:49:33.640
or context of some kind.

01:49:33.640 --> 01:49:36.420
Now, I've talked about
this on the podcast before,

01:49:36.420 --> 01:49:37.880
at least, I think I have.

01:49:37.880 --> 01:49:41.040
And the way this works has been defined

01:49:41.040 --> 01:49:44.110
largely by the wonderful
work of Susumu Tonegawa

01:49:44.110 --> 01:49:47.380
at Massachusetts Institute
of Technology, MIT.

01:49:47.380 --> 01:49:50.690
Susumu collected a Nobel
Prize, quite appropriately,

01:49:50.690 --> 01:49:52.970
for his beautiful work on immunology.

01:49:52.970 --> 01:49:55.680
And he's also a highly
accomplished neuroscientist

01:49:55.680 --> 01:49:58.880
who studies memory and
learning and deja vu.

01:49:58.880 --> 01:50:01.080
And I should also mention
the beautiful work

01:50:01.080 --> 01:50:02.850
of Mark Mayford at the Scripps Institute

01:50:02.850 --> 01:50:04.640
and UC San Diego.

01:50:04.640 --> 01:50:06.730
Beautiful work on this notion of deja vu.

01:50:06.730 --> 01:50:08.760
Here's what they discovered.

01:50:08.760 --> 01:50:11.260
They evaluated the
patterns of neural firing

01:50:11.260 --> 01:50:15.640
in the hippocampus as
subjects learn new things.

01:50:15.640 --> 01:50:20.050
Okay, so neuron A fires,
then neuron B fires,

01:50:20.050 --> 01:50:22.870
then neuron C fires in
a particular sequence.

01:50:22.870 --> 01:50:25.830
Again, the firing of neurons
in a particular sequence

01:50:25.830 --> 01:50:27.530
like the playing of keys on a piano

01:50:27.530 --> 01:50:28.700
in a particular sequence leads

01:50:28.700 --> 01:50:30.050
to a particular song on the piano

01:50:30.050 --> 01:50:32.150
and leads to a particular memory

01:50:32.150 --> 01:50:34.053
of an experience within the brain.

01:50:35.330 --> 01:50:39.260
They then used some
molecular tools and tricks

01:50:39.260 --> 01:50:41.980
to label and capture those neurons

01:50:41.980 --> 01:50:44.020
such that they could go back later

01:50:44.020 --> 01:50:46.330
and activate those neurons in either

01:50:46.330 --> 01:50:49.860
the same sequence or
in a different sequence

01:50:49.860 --> 01:50:51.780
to the one that occurred during

01:50:51.780 --> 01:50:53.820
the formation of the memory.

01:50:53.820 --> 01:50:55.370
To make a long story short,

01:50:55.370 --> 01:50:57.340
and to summarize multiple papers

01:50:57.340 --> 01:51:00.660
published in incredibly
high tier journals,

01:51:00.660 --> 01:51:02.100
journals like Nature and Science

01:51:02.100 --> 01:51:03.830
which are extremely stringent,

01:51:03.830 --> 01:51:08.830
found that whether or not
those particular neurons

01:51:08.960 --> 01:51:10.720
were played in the precise sequence

01:51:10.720 --> 01:51:13.420
that happened when they encoded the memory

01:51:13.420 --> 01:51:14.790
or whether or not those neurons

01:51:14.790 --> 01:51:16.690
were played in a different sequence,

01:51:16.690 --> 01:51:19.610
or even if those neurons were played,

01:51:19.610 --> 01:51:22.370
activated that is, all at once

01:51:22.370 --> 01:51:24.910
with no temporal sequence.

01:51:24.910 --> 01:51:27.543
All firing in concert all at once,

01:51:28.610 --> 01:51:31.750
evoked the same behavior.

01:51:31.750 --> 01:51:35.010
And in some sense, the same memory.

01:51:35.010 --> 01:51:38.960
So at a neural circuit
level, this is deja vu.

01:51:38.960 --> 01:51:41.330
This is a different pattern of firing

01:51:41.330 --> 01:51:43.120
of neurons in the brain leading

01:51:43.120 --> 01:51:46.260
to the same sense of what happened,

01:51:46.260 --> 01:51:51.260
leading to a particular
emotional state or behavior.

01:51:51.330 --> 01:51:54.230
Whether or not this same
sort of phenomenon occurs

01:51:54.230 --> 01:51:55.450
when you're walking down the street

01:51:55.450 --> 01:51:57.470
and suddenly you feel as if, wow,

01:51:57.470 --> 01:51:58.610
I feel like I've been here before.

01:51:58.610 --> 01:51:59.940
You meet someone and you feel like,

01:51:59.940 --> 01:52:01.240
gosh I feel like I know you.

01:52:01.240 --> 01:52:03.380
I feel like there's some familiarity here

01:52:03.380 --> 01:52:04.800
that I can't quite put my finger on.

01:52:04.800 --> 01:52:07.720
We don't know for sure
that's what's happening

01:52:07.720 --> 01:52:10.210
but this is the most mechanistic

01:52:10.210 --> 01:52:12.660
and logical explanation for what

01:52:12.660 --> 01:52:15.800
has for many decades, if
not hundreds of years,

01:52:15.800 --> 01:52:17.700
has been described as deja vu.

01:52:17.700 --> 01:52:20.520
So for those of you that
experience deja vu often,

01:52:20.520 --> 01:52:23.720
just know that this
reflects a normal pattern

01:52:23.720 --> 01:52:26.000
of encoding experiences and events

01:52:26.000 --> 01:52:27.253
within your hippocampus.

01:52:28.198 --> 01:52:30.870
I'm not aware of any
pathological situations

01:52:30.870 --> 01:52:34.850
where the presence of deja
vu inhibits daily life.

01:52:34.850 --> 01:52:37.460
Some people like the sensation of deja vu.

01:52:37.460 --> 01:52:38.670
Other people don't.

01:52:38.670 --> 01:52:40.480
Almost everybody, however, describes it

01:52:40.480 --> 01:52:41.860
as somewhat eerie.

01:52:41.860 --> 01:52:43.350
This idea that even though you're in

01:52:43.350 --> 01:52:45.190
a very different place, even
though you're interacting

01:52:45.190 --> 01:52:48.830
with a very different person,
that you could somehow

01:52:48.830 --> 01:52:51.020
feel as if this has happened before.

01:52:51.020 --> 01:52:53.810
And just realize this,
that your hippocampus,

01:52:53.810 --> 01:52:57.730
while it is exquisitely good at encoding

01:52:57.730 --> 01:53:01.930
new types of perceptions, new experiences,

01:53:01.930 --> 01:53:05.670
new emotions, new
contingencies and relationships

01:53:05.670 --> 01:53:08.960
of life events, it is not infinitely large

01:53:08.960 --> 01:53:12.280
nor does it have an infinite bucket full

01:53:12.280 --> 01:53:14.260
of different options
of different sequences

01:53:14.260 --> 01:53:15.580
for those neurons to play.

01:53:15.580 --> 01:53:17.870
So in a lot of ways it makes perfect sense

01:53:17.870 --> 01:53:19.710
that sometimes we would feel as if

01:53:19.710 --> 01:53:22.140
a given experience had
happened previously.

01:53:22.140 --> 01:53:24.130
I'd like to cover one additional tool

01:53:24.130 --> 01:53:26.420
that you can use to improve
learning and memory.

01:53:26.420 --> 01:53:29.520
And I should mention, this is
a particularly powerful one

01:53:29.520 --> 01:53:33.463
and it's one that I'm definitely
going to employ myself.

01:53:34.350 --> 01:53:36.890
This is based on a paper from none other

01:53:36.890 --> 01:53:39.410
than Wendy Suzuki at New York University.

01:53:39.410 --> 01:53:41.020
We talked about her a little bit earlier.

01:53:41.020 --> 01:53:42.940
And again, she's going
to be on the podcast

01:53:42.940 --> 01:53:45.060
in our next episode.

01:53:45.060 --> 01:53:46.420
And is just an incredible researcher.

01:53:46.420 --> 01:53:47.860
I've known Wendy for a number of years

01:53:47.860 --> 01:53:49.640
and it's only in the
last, I would say five

01:53:49.640 --> 01:53:51.170
or six years that she's really shifted

01:53:51.170 --> 01:53:54.770
her laboratory toward generating protocols

01:53:54.770 --> 01:53:56.130
that human beings can use.

01:53:56.130 --> 01:53:58.630
And she's putting that to great effect,

01:53:58.630 --> 01:54:00.460
great positive effect I should say.

01:54:00.460 --> 01:54:02.010
Publishing papers of the sort

01:54:02.010 --> 01:54:02.843
that I'm about to describe.

01:54:02.843 --> 01:54:04.900
But also incorporating some of these tools

01:54:04.900 --> 01:54:07.290
and protocols into the learning curriculum

01:54:07.290 --> 01:54:10.410
and the lifestyle curriculum
of students at NYU.

01:54:10.410 --> 01:54:12.550
Which I think is a terrific initiative.

01:54:12.550 --> 01:54:14.060
So you don't need to be an NYU student

01:54:14.060 --> 01:54:15.640
in order to benefit from her work.

01:54:15.640 --> 01:54:17.140
I'm going to tell you
about some of that work now

01:54:17.140 --> 01:54:19.790
and she'll tell you
about this and much more

01:54:19.790 --> 01:54:21.670
in the episode that follows this one.

01:54:21.670 --> 01:54:24.010
The title of this paper
will tell you a lot

01:54:24.010 --> 01:54:24.950
about where we're going.

01:54:24.950 --> 01:54:27.630
The title is Brief Daily
Meditation Enhances

01:54:27.630 --> 01:54:31.240
Attention, Memory, Mood,
and Emotional Regulation

01:54:31.240 --> 01:54:33.920
in Non-Experienced Meditators.

01:54:33.920 --> 01:54:36.073
If ever there was an
incentive to meditate,

01:54:37.040 --> 01:54:40.554
it is the data contained
within this paper.

01:54:40.554 --> 01:54:41.910
I want to briefly describe the study

01:54:41.910 --> 01:54:44.450
and then I also want to emphasize

01:54:44.450 --> 01:54:47.540
that when you meditate
is absolutely critical.

01:54:47.540 --> 01:54:49.600
I'll talk about that just at the end.

01:54:49.600 --> 01:54:54.240
This is a study that involves
subjects aged 18 to 45.

01:54:54.240 --> 01:54:57.533
None of whom were experienced
mediators prior to this study.

01:54:59.170 --> 01:55:01.513
There were two general
groups in this study.

01:55:02.490 --> 01:55:06.830
One group did a 13 minute long meditation

01:55:06.830 --> 01:55:09.980
and this meditation was a
fairly conventional meditation.

01:55:09.980 --> 01:55:11.880
They would sit or lie down.

01:55:11.880 --> 01:55:14.280
They would do somewhat of a body scan,

01:55:14.280 --> 01:55:16.700
evaluating for instance
how tense or relaxed

01:55:16.700 --> 01:55:17.740
they felt throughout their body

01:55:17.740 --> 01:55:19.610
and they would focus on their breathing.

01:55:19.610 --> 01:55:21.520
Trying to bring their attention
back to their breathing

01:55:21.520 --> 01:55:25.233
and to the state of their body
as the meditation progressed.

01:55:26.130 --> 01:55:28.770
The other group, which we
can call the control group

01:55:28.770 --> 01:55:31.700
listened to of all things, a podcast.

01:55:31.700 --> 01:55:33.520
They did not listen to this podcast.

01:55:33.520 --> 01:55:36.640
They listened to Radio Lab,
which is a popular podcast,

01:55:36.640 --> 01:55:38.560
for an equivalent amount of time.

01:55:38.560 --> 01:55:40.130
But they were not
instructed to do any kind

01:55:40.130 --> 01:55:43.340
of body scan or pay
attention to their breathing.

01:55:43.340 --> 01:55:46.270
Every subject in the study
either meditated daily

01:55:46.270 --> 01:55:49.460
or listened to a equivalent
duration podcast daily

01:55:49.460 --> 01:55:51.880
for a period of eight weeks.

01:55:51.880 --> 01:55:54.799
And the experimenters
measured a large number

01:55:54.799 --> 01:55:58.050
of things, of variables, as we say.

01:55:58.050 --> 01:56:00.560
They looked at measures
of emotion regulation.

01:56:00.560 --> 01:56:03.020
They actually measured
cortisol, a stress hormone.

01:56:03.020 --> 01:56:05.250
They measured, as the title suggests,

01:56:05.250 --> 01:56:07.780
attention and memory and so forth.

01:56:07.780 --> 01:56:10.370
And the basic takeaway of this study

01:56:10.370 --> 01:56:13.430
is that eight weeks but not four weeks

01:56:13.430 --> 01:56:16.290
of this daily 13 minute a day mediation

01:56:16.290 --> 01:56:19.940
had a significant effect in improving

01:56:19.940 --> 01:56:23.103
attention, memory, mood,
and emotion regulation.

01:56:24.590 --> 01:56:26.860
I find this study to be very interesting

01:56:26.860 --> 01:56:29.810
and in fact, important because most of us

01:56:29.810 --> 01:56:31.090
have heard about the positive effects

01:56:31.090 --> 01:56:34.120
of meditation on things
like stress reduction.

01:56:34.120 --> 01:56:36.740
Or on things such as improving sleep.

01:56:36.740 --> 01:56:39.260
And I want to come back
to sleep in a few moments

01:56:39.260 --> 01:56:41.130
because it turns out to
be very important feature

01:56:41.130 --> 01:56:41.963
of this study.

01:56:43.190 --> 01:56:45.720
This particular study I like so much

01:56:45.720 --> 01:56:48.370
because they used a really broad array

01:56:48.370 --> 01:56:51.290
of measurements for cognitive function.

01:56:51.290 --> 01:56:53.300
Things like the Wisconsin
Card Sorting Task.

01:56:53.300 --> 01:56:54.280
I'm not going to go into this.

01:56:54.280 --> 01:56:56.390
Things like the Stroop Task and they also,

01:56:56.390 --> 01:56:58.730
as I mentioned, measured cortisol.

01:56:58.730 --> 01:57:03.310
And many other things, including,
not surprisingly, memory.

01:57:03.310 --> 01:57:06.160
And people's ability to
remember certain types

01:57:06.160 --> 01:57:08.840
of information, in fact
varied types of information.

01:57:08.840 --> 01:57:11.780
And the basic takeaway was, again,

01:57:11.780 --> 01:57:14.220
that you could get really
robust improvements

01:57:14.220 --> 01:57:16.770
in learning and memory, mood and attention

01:57:16.770 --> 01:57:19.650
from just 13 minutes a day of meditation.

01:57:19.650 --> 01:57:21.410
Now there's an important
twist in this study

01:57:21.410 --> 01:57:22.810
that I want to emphasize.

01:57:22.810 --> 01:57:25.300
If you read into the
discussion of the study

01:57:25.300 --> 01:57:28.730
it's mentioned that somehow meditation

01:57:28.730 --> 01:57:32.370
did not improve but actually
impaired sleep quality

01:57:32.370 --> 01:57:33.937
compared to the control subjects.

01:57:33.937 --> 01:57:36.120
And you might think,
wow, why would that be?

01:57:36.120 --> 01:57:38.900
I mean, meditation is
supposed to reduce our stress.

01:57:38.900 --> 01:57:40.730
Stress is supposed to inhibit sleep.

01:57:40.730 --> 01:57:44.490
And therefore why would sleep get worse?

01:57:44.490 --> 01:57:47.070
Well, what's interesting
is the time of day

01:57:47.070 --> 01:57:48.960
when most of these subjects tended

01:57:48.960 --> 01:57:51.240
to do their meditation.

01:57:51.240 --> 01:57:52.720
Most of the subjects in this study

01:57:52.720 --> 01:57:54.450
did their meditation late in the day.

01:57:54.450 --> 01:57:56.110
This is often the case in experiments.

01:57:56.110 --> 01:57:58.290
I know this because we run experiments

01:57:58.290 --> 01:57:59.870
with human subjects in my laboratory

01:57:59.870 --> 01:58:02.070
and people are paid some amount of money

01:58:02.070 --> 01:58:04.720
in order to participate
or they're given something

01:58:04.720 --> 01:58:06.140
as compensation for being in the study.

01:58:06.140 --> 01:58:08.530
But often times the meditation,

01:58:08.530 --> 01:58:11.010
or in the case of my
lab, the respiration work

01:58:11.010 --> 01:58:13.630
or other kinds of things
that they're assigned to do

01:58:13.630 --> 01:58:15.520
are not their top, top priority.

01:58:15.520 --> 01:58:16.630
And we understand this.

01:58:16.630 --> 01:58:18.600
But in this study, the
majority of subjects here

01:58:18.600 --> 01:58:21.360
I'm reading completed
their meditation sessions

01:58:21.360 --> 01:58:24.500
from somewhere between 8:00 and 11:00 P.M.

01:58:24.500 --> 01:58:27.690
And sometimes even between
12:00 and 3:00 A.M.

01:58:27.690 --> 01:58:28.640
I think there probably were a lot

01:58:28.640 --> 01:58:30.510
of college students
enrolled in this study.

01:58:30.510 --> 01:58:33.680
And their hours often are late shifted.

01:58:33.680 --> 01:58:35.137
That impaired sleep.

01:58:35.137 --> 01:58:37.800
And this raises a bigger theme
that I think is important.

01:58:37.800 --> 01:58:39.760
Many times before on this podcast

01:58:39.760 --> 01:58:40.710
and certainly in the episode

01:58:40.710 --> 01:58:44.620
on mastering sleep and
conquering or mastering stress

01:58:44.620 --> 01:58:46.870
those episodes we talked
about the value, again,

01:58:46.870 --> 01:58:49.870
of these non-sleep deep
rest protocols, NSDR,

01:58:49.870 --> 01:58:52.470
for reducing the activity

01:58:52.470 --> 01:58:54.010
of your sympathetic nervous system.

01:58:54.010 --> 01:58:56.450
The alertness, so-called stress arm

01:58:56.450 --> 01:58:58.570
of your autonomic nervous system.

01:58:58.570 --> 01:59:00.500
The one that makes you feel really alert.

01:59:00.500 --> 01:59:04.450
NSDR is superb for reducing
your level of alertness,

01:59:04.450 --> 01:59:05.820
increasing your level of calmness,

01:59:05.820 --> 01:59:06.960
and putting you into a so-called more

01:59:06.960 --> 01:59:09.900
parasympathetic, relaxed state.

01:59:09.900 --> 01:59:14.240
Meditation does that too, but
it also increases attention.

01:59:14.240 --> 01:59:16.290
If you think about meditation,

01:59:16.290 --> 01:59:18.610
meditation involves
focusing on your breath

01:59:18.610 --> 01:59:20.650
and constantly focusing
back on your breath

01:59:20.650 --> 01:59:22.100
and trying to avoid the distraction

01:59:22.100 --> 01:59:24.300
of things you're thinking or
things that you're hearing.

01:59:24.300 --> 01:59:26.680
And coming, so-called, back to your body,

01:59:26.680 --> 01:59:28.050
back to your breath.

01:59:28.050 --> 01:59:30.500
So meditation is actually,

01:59:30.500 --> 01:59:33.120
it has a high attentional load.

01:59:33.120 --> 01:59:35.780
It requires a lot of
prefrontal cortical activity

01:59:35.780 --> 01:59:37.150
that's involved in attention.

01:59:37.150 --> 01:59:40.710
Which then logically relates

01:59:40.710 --> 01:59:42.870
to one of the outcomes of this study

01:59:42.870 --> 01:59:44.600
which is that attention ability

01:59:44.600 --> 01:59:46.863
is improved in daily meditators.

01:59:47.730 --> 01:59:49.580
It also points out that increasing

01:59:49.580 --> 01:59:51.440
the level of attention and the activity

01:59:51.440 --> 01:59:53.090
of your prefrontal cortex may,

01:59:53.090 --> 01:59:54.010
and I want to emphasize may,

01:59:54.010 --> 01:59:56.570
because here I'm speculating
about the underlying mechanism,

01:59:56.570 --> 01:59:59.120
inhibit your ability to fall asleep.

01:59:59.120 --> 02:00:01.180
So while we have
meditation on the one hand

02:00:01.180 --> 02:00:03.090
that does tend to put us into a calm state

02:00:03.090 --> 02:00:05.600
but it is a calm, very focused state.

02:00:05.600 --> 02:00:08.170
In fact, attention and focus are inherent

02:00:08.170 --> 02:00:10.080
to most forms of meditation.

02:00:10.080 --> 02:00:12.780
Non-sleep deep rest, such as Yoga Nidra

02:00:12.780 --> 02:00:14.940
as some of you know it to be.

02:00:14.940 --> 02:00:18.090
Or NSDR, there's a terrific NSDR script

02:00:18.090 --> 02:00:20.067
that's available free online
that's put out by Madefor.

02:00:20.067 --> 02:00:22.440
So you can go to YouTube, NSDR, Madefor.

02:00:22.440 --> 02:00:24.613
You can also go do a search for NSDR.

02:00:24.613 --> 02:00:26.950
There's a number of these
available out there,

02:00:26.950 --> 02:00:28.920
again, at no cost.

02:00:28.920 --> 02:00:32.370
Those NSDR protocols tend to put people

02:00:32.370 --> 02:00:34.670
into a state of deep relaxation

02:00:34.670 --> 02:00:38.010
but also very low attention.

02:00:38.010 --> 02:00:41.200
And we have to assume very low activation

02:00:41.200 --> 02:00:42.700
of the prefrontal cortex.

02:00:42.700 --> 02:00:44.640
So the takeaways from this
study are several fold.

02:00:44.640 --> 02:00:46.840
First of all, that daily meditation

02:00:46.840 --> 02:00:49.610
of 13 minutes can enhance your ability

02:00:49.610 --> 02:00:51.330
to pay attention and to learn.

02:00:51.330 --> 02:00:54.430
It can truly enhance memory.

02:00:54.430 --> 02:00:57.970
However, you need to do that
for at least eight weeks

02:00:57.970 --> 02:01:00.700
in order to start to
see the effects to occur

02:01:00.700 --> 02:01:02.940
and we have to presume
that you have to continue

02:01:02.940 --> 02:01:05.313
those meditation training sessions.

02:01:06.360 --> 02:01:08.780
In fact, they found
that if people only did

02:01:08.780 --> 02:01:11.070
four weeks of meditation
these effects didn't show up.

02:01:11.070 --> 02:01:13.070
Now eight weeks might
seem like a long time,

02:01:13.070 --> 02:01:14.650
but I think that 13 minutes a day

02:01:14.650 --> 02:01:18.540
is not actually that big
of a time commitment.

02:01:18.540 --> 02:01:20.620
And the results of this study certainly

02:01:20.620 --> 02:01:23.260
incentivize me to start adopting a,

02:01:23.260 --> 02:01:24.910
I'm going for 15 minutes a day now.

02:01:24.910 --> 02:01:27.770
I've been an on and off
meditator for a number of years.

02:01:27.770 --> 02:01:29.200
I've been pretty good about it lately,

02:01:29.200 --> 02:01:31.970
but I confess I've been
doing far shorter meditations

02:01:31.970 --> 02:01:34.710
of anywhere from three to
five, or maybe 10 minutes.

02:01:34.710 --> 02:01:37.600
I'm going to ramp that
up to 15 minutes a day.

02:01:37.600 --> 02:01:39.560
And I'm doing that specifically to try

02:01:39.560 --> 02:01:42.310
and access these improvements
in cognitive ability

02:01:42.310 --> 02:01:43.740
and our abilities to learn.

02:01:43.740 --> 02:01:45.790
Also based on the data in this paper,

02:01:45.790 --> 02:01:47.530
I'm going to do those meditation sessions

02:01:47.530 --> 02:01:51.650
either early in the day, such
as immediately after waking,

02:01:51.650 --> 02:01:52.880
or close to it.

02:01:52.880 --> 02:01:54.390
So I might get my sunshine first.

02:01:54.390 --> 02:01:56.610
I'm, as you all know, very
big on getting sunlight

02:01:56.610 --> 02:01:57.970
in the eyes early in the day.

02:01:57.970 --> 02:02:00.303
As much as one can and
as early as one can.

02:02:01.330 --> 02:02:02.740
Once the sun is out.

02:02:02.740 --> 02:02:04.950
But certainly doing it early in the day

02:02:04.950 --> 02:02:07.690
and not past 5:00 P.M. or so

02:02:07.690 --> 02:02:09.960
in order to make sure that
I don't inhibit sleep.

02:02:09.960 --> 02:02:11.980
Because I think this, the
result that they describe

02:02:11.980 --> 02:02:14.910
of meditation inhibiting quality sleep

02:02:14.910 --> 02:02:17.940
compared to controls is an important one

02:02:17.940 --> 02:02:19.100
to pay attention to.

02:02:19.100 --> 02:02:20.200
No pun intended.

02:02:20.200 --> 02:02:22.550
Today we covered a lot
of aspects of memory

02:02:22.550 --> 02:02:24.510
and how to improve your memory.

02:02:24.510 --> 02:02:26.300
We talked about the
different forms of memory

02:02:26.300 --> 02:02:28.010
and we talked about some of the underlying

02:02:28.010 --> 02:02:30.400
neural circuitry of memory formation

02:02:30.400 --> 02:02:34.060
and we talked about the emotional saliency

02:02:34.060 --> 02:02:36.480
and intensity of what
you're trying to learn

02:02:36.480 --> 02:02:39.730
has a profound impact on
whether or not you learn

02:02:39.730 --> 02:02:42.010
in response to some sort of experience.

02:02:42.010 --> 02:02:44.570
Whether or not that experience is reading,

02:02:44.570 --> 02:02:49.050
or mathematics, or music, or
language, or a physical skill.

02:02:49.050 --> 02:02:50.290
It doesn't matter.

02:02:50.290 --> 02:02:54.120
The more intense of an
emotional state that you're in

02:02:54.120 --> 02:02:57.070
in the period immediately
following that learning,

02:02:57.070 --> 02:02:59.460
the more likely you are to remember

02:02:59.460 --> 02:03:01.470
whatever it is that
you're trying to learn.

02:03:01.470 --> 02:03:02.830
And we talked about the neuro chemicals

02:03:02.830 --> 02:03:04.600
that explain that effect.

02:03:04.600 --> 02:03:08.370
About epinephrine and
corticosterones like cortisol.

02:03:08.370 --> 02:03:10.620
And how adjusting the timing of those

02:03:10.620 --> 02:03:13.330
is so key to enhancing your memory.

02:03:13.330 --> 02:03:14.630
And we talked about the different ways

02:03:14.630 --> 02:03:15.700
to enhance those chemicals.

02:03:15.700 --> 02:03:19.230
Everything ranging from
cold water to pharmacology

02:03:19.230 --> 02:03:21.630
and even just adjusting
the emotional state

02:03:21.630 --> 02:03:24.260
within your mind in order to stamp down

02:03:24.260 --> 02:03:26.120
and remember experiences better.

02:03:26.120 --> 02:03:28.180
We also talked about how
to leverage exercise,

02:03:28.180 --> 02:03:31.730
in particular, load bearing exercise

02:03:31.730 --> 02:03:33.930
in order to evoke the release of hormones

02:03:33.930 --> 02:03:35.480
like osteocalcin which can travel

02:03:35.480 --> 02:03:37.100
from your bones to your brain and enhance

02:03:37.100 --> 02:03:38.550
your ability to learn.

02:03:38.550 --> 02:03:41.040
And we talked about a new
form of photographic memory.

02:03:41.040 --> 02:03:43.820
Not the traditional type
of photographic memory

02:03:43.820 --> 02:03:45.340
in which people can remember everything

02:03:45.340 --> 02:03:47.200
they look at very easily.

02:03:47.200 --> 02:03:49.160
But rather, taking mental snapshots

02:03:49.160 --> 02:03:50.500
of things that you see.

02:03:50.500 --> 02:03:52.610
Again, emphasizing that will create

02:03:52.610 --> 02:03:54.280
a better memory of what you see

02:03:54.280 --> 02:03:55.600
when you take that mental snapshot,

02:03:55.600 --> 02:03:57.650
but will actually reduce your memory

02:03:57.650 --> 02:03:59.680
for the things that you
hear at that moment.

02:03:59.680 --> 02:04:01.290
And we discussed the really exciting data

02:04:01.290 --> 02:04:04.010
looking at how particular
meditation protocols

02:04:04.010 --> 02:04:07.700
can enhance memory but
also attention and mood.

02:04:07.700 --> 02:04:10.800
However, if done too late in the day,

02:04:10.800 --> 02:04:13.590
can actually disrupt
sleep precisely because

02:04:13.590 --> 02:04:17.380
those meditation protocols
can enhance attention.

02:04:17.380 --> 02:04:19.632
Now I know that many of you are interested

02:04:19.632 --> 02:04:22.420
in neuro chemicals that can
enhance learning and memory.

02:04:22.420 --> 02:04:26.640
And I intend to cover those in deep detail

02:04:26.640 --> 02:04:28.040
in a future episode.

02:04:28.040 --> 02:04:30.290
However, for the sake of
what was discussed today,

02:04:30.290 --> 02:04:33.330
please understand that any number

02:04:33.330 --> 02:04:35.100
of different neuro chemicals can evoke

02:04:35.100 --> 02:04:37.330
or can increase the amount of adrenaline

02:04:37.330 --> 02:04:39.630
that's circulating in your brain and body.

02:04:39.630 --> 02:04:43.170
And it's less important how one accesses

02:04:43.170 --> 02:04:45.020
that increase in adrenaline.

02:04:45.020 --> 02:04:46.930
Again, this can be done
through behavioral protocols

02:04:46.930 --> 02:04:48.283
or through pharmacology.

02:04:49.480 --> 02:04:51.860
Assuming that those behavioral protocols

02:04:51.860 --> 02:04:53.900
and pharmacology are safe for you,

02:04:53.900 --> 02:04:55.810
it really doesn't matter how you evoke

02:04:55.810 --> 02:04:57.750
the adrenaline release because remember,

02:04:57.750 --> 02:05:00.190
adrenaline is the final common pathway

02:05:00.190 --> 02:05:03.540
by which particular experiences,
particular perceptions

02:05:03.540 --> 02:05:05.670
are stamped into memory.

02:05:05.670 --> 02:05:08.120
Which answers our very
first question raised

02:05:08.120 --> 02:05:09.330
at the beginning of the episode.

02:05:09.330 --> 02:05:12.140
Which is, why do we
remember anything at all?

02:05:12.140 --> 02:05:14.290
Right, that was the
question that we raised.

02:05:14.290 --> 02:05:16.220
Why is it that from morning 'til night

02:05:16.220 --> 02:05:17.930
throughout your entire life you have tons

02:05:17.930 --> 02:05:19.750
of sensory experience,
tons of perceptions.

02:05:19.750 --> 02:05:21.880
Why is it that some are remembered

02:05:21.880 --> 02:05:23.190
and others are not?

02:05:23.190 --> 02:05:25.440
While I would never want to distill

02:05:25.440 --> 02:05:27.480
an important question such as that down

02:05:27.480 --> 02:05:29.870
to a one molecule type of answer,

02:05:29.870 --> 02:05:32.300
I think we can confidently say based on

02:05:32.300 --> 02:05:36.180
the vast amount of animal
and human research data

02:05:36.180 --> 02:05:38.610
that epinephrine, adrenaline,

02:05:38.610 --> 02:05:39.940
and some of the other chemicals

02:05:39.940 --> 02:05:43.520
that it acts with in concert, is in fact,

02:05:43.520 --> 02:05:47.200
the way that we remember particular events

02:05:47.200 --> 02:05:49.060
and not all events.

